which forms a geometric series that can be subsequently summed to yield

$$ \langle\sigma(x)\sigma(y)\rangle=\frac{({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1)}{2\pi^{\alpha+1}}\frac{\Gamma(\alpha)}{\Gamma(2\alpha)}\oint_{\mathcal{C}}\frac{\mathrm{d}J}{2\pi i}\,\frac{\Gamma({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}J)\Gamma(J+2\alpha)(J+\alpha)}{(J+\Delta_{\sigma})(J+\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma})-\Pi_{\sigma}(J)}\,{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\minus J, \,\, J+2 \alpha$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$\alpha+ \tfrac{1}{2}$}}\,\bigg|\,\frac{1+\xi}{2}\,\right]\,. $$

Following [35], the asymptotic behavior of (3.5) at future infinity and fixed spatial separation, $\xi\to{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\infty$ , and thus the physical mass of the field,1111 11 This is familiar from quantum field theory in flat space, where the poles of the momentum space propagator $G(k)=1/(k^{2}+m^{2}-\Pi(k^{2}))$ , with $\Pi(k^{2})$ the self-energy, control the long-distance behavior of the position space propagator, and thus provides a physical notion of mass in the interacting theory. is controlled by the pole $J_{*}$ of the integrand,

$$ (J_{*}+\Delta_{\sigma})(J_{*}+\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma})-\Pi_{\sigma}(J_{*})=0\,, $$

with maximal real part.

For a weakly interacting heavy field $\sigma$ , there is a pair of poles with largest real part, one the complex conjugate of the other, which we denote as $J_{*}$ and $\bar{J}_{*}$ . At future infinity, the heavy field propagator thus behaves as $\langle\sigma(x)\sigma(y)\rangle\sim\mathcal{C}_{1}\hskip 0.5pt({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}2\xi)^{J_{*}}+\mathcal{C}_{2}\hskip 0.5pt({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}2\xi)^{\bar{J}_{*}}$ as $\xi\to{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\infty$ for some constants $\mathcal{C}_{1}$ and $\mathcal{C}_{2}$ . For free fields, $\Pi_{\sigma}(J)=0$ and so $J_{*}={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\sigma}$ and $\bar{J}_{*}={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}$ , while at leading order in perturbation theory the non-zero self-energy shifts the pole to

$$ J_{*}\approx-\Delta_{\sigma}-\frac{\Pi_{\sigma}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\sigma})}{\Delta_{\sigma}-\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}}\,, $$

with $\bar{J}_{*}$ given by the conjugate of this expression, or by taking $\Delta_{\sigma}\to\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}$ . Specifically, decomposing the pole into its real and imaginary parts,

$$ J_{*}\approx-\!\!\left[\alpha+\frac{{\mathrm{Im}}\,\Pi_{\sigma}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\sigma})}{2\sqrt{m_{\sigma}^{2}-\alpha^{2}}}\right]-i\left[\sqrt{m_{\sigma}^{2}-\alpha^{2}}+\frac{{\mathrm{Re}}\,\Pi_{\sigma}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\sigma})}{2\sqrt{m_{\sigma}^{2}-\alpha^{2}}}\right]\,. $$

we find that the real part of the self-energy affects the physically-measured mass of $\sigma$ , while its imaginary part changes the decay of the correlator at long distances. We will work in an “on-shell” mass renormalization scheme in which $m_{\sigma}$ is $\sigma$ ’s physically-measured mass, and so we adjust our counterterms so that

$$ {\mathrm{Im}}\,J_{*}={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\sqrt{m_{\sigma}^{2}-\alpha^{2}}\,, $$

or equivalently, to first order in perturbation theory, ${\mathrm{Re}}\,\Pi_{\sigma}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\sigma})=0$ . The shift in $J_{*}$ ’s real part is a purely physical effect that cannot be mimicked in free field theory. Furthermore, to ensure that the two-point function is properly normalized in the long-distance limit, we require that the residue of this pole is unchanged and thus $\Pi_{\sigma}^{\prime}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\sigma})=0$ .

A weakly interacting light field $\varphi$ only has one pole with largest real part, which we again denote as $J_{*}$ . As for the heavy field, $J_{*}=-\Delta_{\varphi}$ in the free theory, while at leading order in perturbation theory a non-zero self-energy $\Pi_{\varphi}(J)$ corrects this to

$$ J_{*}\approx-\Delta_{\varphi}-\frac{\Pi_{\varphi}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\varphi})}{\Delta_{\varphi}-\bar{\Delta}_{\varphi}}\,. $$

We will find that $\Pi_{\varphi}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\varphi})$ is purely real. If we define the physically-measured mass of a light field by $J_{*}\equiv-\alpha+\sqrt{\alpha^{2}-m_{\smash{\varphi}}^{2}}$ , thus working in an on-shell renormalization scheme, this requires that we choose our counterterms such that $\Pi_{\varphi}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\varphi})=0$ . So, unlike the heavy field, a non-zero self-energy $\Pi_{\sigma}(J)$ does not result in a measurable effect as $\xi\to{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\infty$ .

Finally, before we move on to our specific examples, it will be useful to discuss the general strategy we will use for simplifying self-energy corrections in the limit $m_{\varphi}\to 0$ . The self-energies we study will be expressed in terms of the inversion formula (2.22) applied to products of $\sigma$ and $\varphi$ propagators. As discussed in §2.2, the free field propagator can be decomposed into a sum (2.19) of terms with definite scaling behavior in the infrared,

$$ G(\zeta)=\mathcal{G}_{\Delta}(\zeta)+\mathcal{G}_{\bar{\Delta}}(\zeta)\sim\mathcal{A}(\Delta)({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1/\zeta)^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}\Delta}+\mathcal{A}(\bar{\Delta})({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1/\zeta)^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}\bar{\Delta}}\,,\mathrlap{\quad\zeta\to 0\,,} $$

where $\mathcal{A}(\Delta)\propto\Gamma(\Delta)\sim\Delta^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}1}$ as $\Delta\to 0$ is given by (2.20). Our self-energies will then be sums of terms like $[\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\sigma}}]_{J}$ and $[\mathcal{G}_{\smash{\bar{\Delta}_{\varphi}}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\sigma}}]_{J}$ . As we will show in this section, and as we might expect from our analysis of the propagator in §2.3, a term like $[\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\sigma}}]_{J}$ contributes “potential” poles to the self-energy at $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}(\Delta_{\varphi}+\Delta_{\sigma}+k)$ , with $k\in\mathbb{N}$ , whose residues may be zero (i.e. the so-called “spurious poles” of [59]) but are proportional to $\mathcal{A}(\Delta_{\varphi})\mathcal{A}(\Delta_{\sigma})\propto\Gamma(\Delta_{\varphi})\Gamma(\Delta_{\sigma})$ .

Since we are interested in how the light scalar $\varphi$ affects the long-distance behavior of the heavy scalar $\sigma$ , we want to approximate $\Pi_{\sigma}(J)$ near the free-field pole $J_{*}={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\sigma}$ . As $m_{\varphi}\to 0$ , the dimension of $\varphi$ also vanishes, $\Delta_{\varphi}\sim m_{\varphi}^{2}/(2\alpha)\to 0$ , and so we find that there are two simplifications of the self-energy in this limit. The first is that the terms with factors of $\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}\propto\Gamma(\Delta_{\varphi})\propto m_{\varphi}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}2}$ are enhanced compared to those with $\bar{\mathcal{G}}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}$ , whose amplitude does not diverge as $m_{\varphi}\to 0$ . The second is that only terms with factors of $\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}$ can contribute poles that approach the free-field pole at $J_{*}={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\sigma}$ as $\Delta_{\varphi}\to 0$ , and there are only finitely many that do. Such poles are further enhanced by a factor of $\Delta_{\varphi}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}1}\propto m_{\varphi}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}2}$ compared to other poles or regular terms in the self-energy. We thus find that the self-energy near the free-field pole, and thus the correction to $\sigma$ ’s long-distance behavior, is governed by a single easily-calculated term as $m_{\varphi}\to 0$ .

We will first illustrate this procedure in the so-called “bubble” diagram in §3.1, which is the leading correction in a theory with a cubic interaction $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{int}}=\tfrac{1}{2}g\varphi\sigma^{2}$ . Here, there are exact analytic results we can use to check our approximations. Then, in §3.2, we study the general theory with quartic interactions with $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{int}}=\tfrac{1}{2}g\sigma^{2}\varphi^{2}+\tfrac{1}{4!}g_{\varphi}\varphi^{4}+\tfrac{1}{4!}g_{\sigma}\sigma^{4}$ , computing the leading order physical corrections to the two-point functions in the limit $m_{\varphi}\to 0$ . Throughout, we will regulate UV divergences by first working with $\alpha=\tfrac{1}{2}(3-\epsilon)$ and then taking $\epsilon\to 0$ .

