or equivalently,

$$ \Pi_{\sigma}(J)=({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}g)^{2}[G^{\sigma}G^{\varphi}]_{J}-J(J+2\alpha)\delta_{Z_{\sigma}}-\delta_{m_{\sigma}}\,, $$

where $[G^{\sigma}G^{\varphi}]_{J}$ is determined by the inversion formula (2.22) applied to the collection of propagators appearing in the loop, $H(\zeta)=G^{\sigma}(\zeta)G^{\varphi}(\zeta)$ . An exact expression for $[G^{\sigma}G^{\varphi}]_{J}$ was found in [35], which we present in (A.15). As we illustrate in Figure 1, they found that the bubble has poles at $J=-\Delta_{\sigma}-\Delta_{\varphi}-2k$ , $J=-\Delta_{\sigma}-\bar{\Delta}_{\varphi}-2k$ , $J=-\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}-\Delta_{\varphi}-2k$ and $J=-\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}-\bar{\Delta}_{\varphi}-2k$ , for all non-negative integer $k\in\mathbb{N}$ . Physically, these correspond to the appearance of long-lived two-particle states with non-zero momenta. Our goal is to see this structure directly from the inversion formula (2.22) and to extract the self-energy’s $m_{\varphi}\to 0$ behavior.

*Figure 1: The analytic structure of the bubble diagram $[G^{\sigma}G^{\varphi}]_{J}$ for $\alpha=\tfrac{3}{2}$ . In [black], we denote the free-field poles at $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\sigma}={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}(\alpha+i\nu_{\sigma})$ and $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}(\alpha-i\nu_{\sigma})$ at which $[G^{\sigma}G^{\varphi}]_{J}$ is regular. It instead has singularities at $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}(\Delta_{\sigma}+\Delta_{\varphi}+2k)$ and $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}(\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}+\Delta_{\varphi}+2k)$ , with $k\in\mathbb{N}$ , in [blue]. These singularities encroach upon the free-field poles and dominate the self-energy as $\Delta_{\varphi}\to 0$ . The bubble also has singularities at $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}(\Delta_{\sigma}+\bar{\Delta}_{\varphi}+2k)$ and $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}(\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}+\bar{\Delta}_{\varphi}+2k)$ , with $k\in\mathbb{N}$ , pictured in [red], which remain well-separated from the free-field poles as $\Delta_{\varphi}\to 0$ .*

Using (2.19), we can expand the bubble into its constituents with definite scaling as $\zeta\to 0$ ,

$$ [G^{\sigma}G^{\varphi}]_{J}=[\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\sigma}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}]_{J}+[\mathcal{G}_{\smash{\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}]_{J}+[\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\sigma}}\mathcal{G}_{\smash{\bar{\Delta}_{\varphi}}}]_{J}+[\mathcal{G}_{\smash{\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}}}\mathcal{G}_{\smash{\bar{\Delta}_{\varphi}}}]_{J}\,.\, $$

We will focus on the first term, $[\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\sigma}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}]_{J}$ , and recover the behavior of the others by simply interchanging the dimensions $\Delta_{\sigma}$ and $\Delta_{\varphi}$ with their shadows $\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}$ and $\bar{\Delta}_{\varphi}$ . The relevant discontinuity is then given by

$$ \displaystyle{\mathrm{disc}}\,\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\sigma}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}(\zeta) \displaystyle=-\frac{2\pi i\hskip 0.5pt\hskip 0.5pt\mathcal{A}(\Delta_{\sigma})\mathcal{A}(\Delta_{\varphi})}{\Gamma(\Delta_{\sigma}+\Delta_{\varphi})\Gamma(1-\Delta_{\sigma}-\Delta_{\varphi})} \displaystyle\times\zeta^{\Delta_{\varphi}+\Delta_{\varphi}}{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\Delta_{\sigma}, \,\, \Delta_\sigma- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 \Delta_\sigma- 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\Delta_{\varphi}, \,\, \Delta_\varphi- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 \Delta_\varphi- 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right] $$

and so the inversion formula reads

$$ \displaystyle{}_{J} \displaystyle=\mathcal{N}_{J,\Delta_{\sigma}\Delta_{\varphi}}\int_{0}^{\infty}\!\mathrm{d}\zeta\,\zeta^{J+\Delta_{\varphi}+\Delta_{\sigma}-1}\,{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$J+\alpha+ \frac{1}{2}, \,\, J+1$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 J + 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right] \displaystyle\times{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\Delta_{\sigma}, \,\, \Delta_\sigma- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 \Delta_\sigma- 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\Delta_{\varphi}, \,\, \Delta_\varphi- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 \Delta_\varphi- 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]\,, $$

with

$$ \mathcal{N}_{J,\Delta_{\sigma}\Delta_{\varphi}}=\frac{2\pi^{\alpha+1}\mathcal{A}(\Delta_{\sigma})\mathcal{A}(\Delta_{\varphi})\Gamma(J+1)}{4^{J}\Gamma(J+\alpha+1)\Gamma(\Delta_{\sigma}+\Delta_{\varphi})\Gamma(1-\Delta_{\sigma}-\Delta_{\varphi})}\,. $$

The coefficients $\mathcal{A}(\Delta)$ are defined in (2.20), and crucially diverge $\mathcal{A}(\Delta)\propto\Delta^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}1}$ as $\Delta\to 0$ . This implies that, barring any enhancements coming from singularities in $J$ , the terms that depend on $\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}$ will be enhanced over those that depend on $\mathcal{G}_{\bar{\Delta}_{\varphi}}$ in the limit that $\Delta_{\varphi}\to 0$ and $\bar{\Delta}_{\varphi}\to\alpha$ .

Following our analysis of the propagator in §2.3, it is convenient to treat the infrared and ultraviolet regions of (3.17) separately, writing $[\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\sigma}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}]_{J}=[\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\sigma}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}]_{J}^{\textsc{ir}}+[\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\sigma}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}]_{J}^{{\textsc{uv}}}$ with

$$ \displaystyle{}_{J}^{{\textsc{ir}}}=\mathcal{N}_{J,\Delta_{\sigma}\Delta_{\varphi}}\int_{0}^{1}\!\mathrm{d}\zeta \displaystyle\zeta^{J+\Delta_{\varphi}+\Delta_{\sigma}-1}\,{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$J+\alpha+ \frac{1}{2}, \,\, J+1$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 J + 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right] \displaystyle\times{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\Delta_{\sigma}, \,\, \Delta_\sigma- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 \Delta_\sigma- 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\Delta_{\varphi}, \,\, \Delta_\varphi- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 \Delta_\varphi- 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]\,. $$

We will focus on this infrared contribution first since we can use it to immediately read off where $[G^{\sigma}G^{\varphi}]_{J}$ ’s singularities are. As $\zeta\to 0$ , the integrand behaves as $\zeta^{J+\Delta_{\varphi}+\Delta_{\sigma}-1}$ and so it diverges when $J=-\Delta_{\varphi}-\Delta_{\sigma}$ . Technically, this integral representation is only well-defined for ${\mathrm{Re}}\,J>-{\mathrm{Re}}\,\Delta_{\varphi}-{\mathrm{Re}}\,\Delta_{\sigma}$ , but we may derive an analytic continuation by series expanding the integrand about $\zeta=0$ ,

$$ \begin{aligned} &{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$J+\alpha+ \frac{1}{2}, \,\, J+1$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 J + 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\Delta_{\sigma}, \,\, \Delta_\sigma- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 \Delta_\sigma- 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]\\
&\qquad\qquad\qquad\times{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\Delta_{\varphi}, \,\, \Delta_\varphi- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 \Delta_\varphi- 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]\end{aligned}\,=\,\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}c_{k}(J,\Delta_{\sigma},\Delta_{\varphi})\hskip 0.5pt\hskip 0.5pt\zeta^{k}\,, $$

and then integrating (3.19) term-by-term to find

$$ [\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\sigma}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}]_{J}^{\textsc{ir}}=\mathcal{N}_{J,\Delta_{\sigma}\Delta_{\varphi}}\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{c_{k}(J,\Delta_{\sigma},\Delta_{\varphi})}{J+\Delta_{\sigma}+\Delta_{\varphi}+k}\,. $$

Immediately, we see that the deep infrared $\zeta\to 0$ of the integral (3.19) generates a family of potential singularities at $J=-\Delta_{\sigma}-\Delta_{\varphi}-k$ , with $k\in\mathbb{N}$ . However, some of their residues may vanish and so some of these poles may be spurious.

Ignoring the coefficient $\mathcal{N}_{J,\Delta_{\sigma}\Delta_{\varphi}}$ for the moment, the residue around each pole $c_{k}({\Delta_{\sigma},\Delta_{\varphi}})\equiv c_{k}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}[\Delta_{\sigma}+\Delta_{\varphi}+k],\Delta_{\sigma},\Delta_{\varphi})$ takes a relatively simple closed form, with non-zero even coefficients

$$ \displaystyle c_{2k}({\Delta_{\sigma},\Delta_{\varphi}})=\frac{1}{2^{4k}k!}\frac{\Gamma(\Delta_{\varphi}+k)}{\Gamma(\Delta_{\varphi})} \displaystyle\Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\alpha+ k\,,\,\Delta_\sigma+k\,,\, \alpha- \Delta_\sigma- k\,,\,\alpha- \Delta_\varphi- k$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$\alpha\,,\,\Delta_\sigma\,,\, \alpha- \Delta_\sigma\,,\,\alpha- \Delta_\varphi$}}\right] \displaystyle\times \displaystyle\Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\Delta_\sigma+ \Delta_\varphi- 2 \alpha+ 1 + 2k \,,\, \Delta_\sigma+ \Delta_\varphi- \alpha+ k$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$\Delta_\sigma+ \Delta_\varphi- 2 \alpha+ 1 +k\,,\, \Delta_\sigma+ \Delta_\varphi- \alpha+ 2k$}}\right] $$

and vanishing odd coefficients, $c_{2k+1}(\Delta_{\varphi},\Delta_{\sigma})=0$ . From this, we find that the bubble indeed has poles at $J=-\Delta_{\sigma}-\Delta_{\varphi}-2k$ , with $k\in\mathbb{N}$ . Furthermore, there is a hierarchy in residues as $\Delta_{\varphi}\to 0$ , with $c_{0}(\Delta_{\sigma},\Delta_{\varphi})=1$ while the rest are $\mathcal{O}(\Delta_{\varphi})$ . We can understand this hierarchy as follows. Since the integral (3.19) is constrained to $\zeta\in[0,1]$ , we may approximate the last hypergeometric function as

$$ {}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\Delta_\varphi, \,\, \Delta_\varphi- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 \Delta_\varphi- 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]=1+\frac{1}{2}\Delta_{\varphi}\,({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta)\,{}_{3}F_{2}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$1\,,\, 1\,,\, \frac{3}{2}- \alpha$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2\,,\, 2 - 2 \alpha$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]+\mathcal{O}(\Delta_{\varphi}^{2})\, $$

and so, to leading order in $\Delta_{\varphi}$ , the integral in (3.19) reduces to that of (2.29), except that the factor of $\zeta^{J+\Delta-1}$ there is now a factor of $\zeta^{J+\Delta_{\sigma}+\Delta_{\varphi}-1}$ , which as we argued there only has a single non-vanishing residue.

Since we are primarily interested in the behavior of the bubble near the free-field pole $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\sigma}$ , to leading order in $\Delta_{\varphi}$ we can approximate

$$ \mathcal{N}_{J,\Delta_{\sigma}\Delta_{\varphi}}\sim\frac{\Gamma(\alpha)}{4\pi^{\alpha+1}}\frac{\mathcal{N}_{J,\Delta_{\sigma}}}{\Delta_{\varphi}}\,,\mathrlap{\qquad\Delta_{\varphi}\to 0\,,} $$

with $\mathcal{N}_{J,\Delta_{\sigma}}$ the coefficient appearing in the inversion of the propagator (2.28), and thus (3.19) as

$$ \displaystyle{}_{J}^{{\textsc{ir}}} \displaystyle\approx\frac{\Gamma(\alpha)}{4\pi^{\alpha+1}}\frac{1}{\Delta_{\varphi}}\frac{\mathcal{N}_{J,\Delta_{\sigma}}}{J+\Delta_{\sigma}+\Delta_{\varphi}}\,. $$

Near the free-field pole $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\sigma}$ , the terms we have dropped are suppressed by a factor of $\Delta_{\varphi}^{2}$ compared to the one we have kept.

The analysis of the UV contribution $[\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\sigma}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}]_{J}^{\textsc{uv}}$ is more involved because it diverges as $\alpha\to\frac{3}{2}$ . We leave its analysis in Appendix D, but the upshot is that $[\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\sigma}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}]_{J}^{{\textsc{uv}}}$ is regular in $J$ and is well-approximated by

$$ \displaystyle{}_{J}^{\textsc{uv}}\approx\frac{\Gamma(\alpha)}{4\pi^{\alpha+1}}\frac{\mathcal{N}_{J,\Delta_{\sigma}}}{\Delta_{\varphi}}\int_{1}^{\infty}\!\mathrm{d}\zeta \displaystyle\zeta^{J+\Delta_{\sigma}-1}\,{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$J+\alpha+ \frac{1}{2}, \,\, J+1$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 J + 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right] \displaystyle\times{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\Delta_{\sigma}, \,\, \Delta_\sigma- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 \Delta_\sigma- 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]+\cdots $$

as $\Delta_{\varphi}\to 0$ , where the $\cdots$ denote terms that diverge as $\epsilon\to 0$ . These divergences simplify drastically only once we sum over dimensions and their shadows, i.e. only in $[G^{\sigma}G^{\varphi}]_{J}$ and not in expressions like $[\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\sigma}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}]_{J}$ . Regardless, aside from the UV divergence, (3.26) is subleading in $\Delta_{\varphi}$ compared to (3.25) near $J=-\Delta_{\sigma}$ and so we may drop it.

A similar story applies to $[\mathcal{G}_{\smash{\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}]_{J}$ , which dominates near the free-field pole at $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}$ , and to compute it we only need to replace $\Delta_{\sigma}$ with $\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}$ in (3.25). The other terms in (3.15) are neither singular near the free-field poles $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\sigma}$ or $J=-\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}$ , nor are they enhanced by diverging factors of $\mathcal{A}(\Delta_{\varphi})$ . Thus, at leading order in $\Delta_{\varphi}$ , we can approximate the bubble as

$$ [G^{\sigma}G^{\varphi}]_{J}\approx[G^{\sigma}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}]_{J}^{\textsc{ir}}+\cdots\approx\frac{\Gamma(\alpha)}{4\pi^{\alpha+1}}\frac{1}{\Delta_{\varphi}}\frac{1}{(J+\Delta_{\sigma}+\Delta_{\varphi})(J+\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}+\Delta_{\varphi})}+\cdots\,, $$

where the $\cdots$ denote the UV divergences from $[G^{\sigma}G^{\varphi}]_{J}^{\textsc{uv}}$ . Specifically, as $\alpha\to\frac{3}{2}$ , this becomes

$$ [G^{\sigma}G^{\varphi}]_{J}\approx\frac{3}{8\pi^{2}}\frac{1}{m_{\varphi}^{2}}\frac{1}{(J+\Delta_{\sigma}+\Delta_{\varphi})(J+\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}+\Delta_{\varphi})}+\frac{1}{8\pi^{2}\epsilon}\,, $$

where the $1/(8\pi^{2}\epsilon)$ is the divergence of the bubble in dimensional regularization (C.13). We compare this approximation to the exact result (A.15) in Figure 2, where we find excellent agreement as $m_{\varphi}\to 0$ . To leading order in $\Delta_{\varphi}$ , we thus find that the self-energy reduces to

$$ \Pi_{\sigma}(J)\approx\frac{g^{2}}{8\pi^{2}\Delta_{\varphi}}\frac{1}{(J+\Delta_{\sigma}+\Delta_{\varphi})(J+\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}+\Delta_{\varphi})}+\frac{g^{2}}{8\pi^{2}\epsilon}-J(J+2\alpha)\delta_{Z_{\sigma}}-\delta_{m_{\sigma}}\, $$

in the regions of $J$ which are relevant for $\sigma$ ’s propagation over long distances. This allows us to determine our counterterms as

$$ \delta_{Z_{\sigma}}=\frac{g^{2}}{32\pi^{2}\nu_{\sigma}^{2}\Delta_{\varphi}^{3}}\quad\text{and}\qquad\delta_{m_{\sigma}}=\frac{g^{2}}{8\pi^{2}\epsilon}+\frac{g^{2}m_{\sigma}^{2}}{32\pi^{2}\nu_{\sigma}^{2}\Delta_{\varphi}^{3}}\,, $$

where $\nu_{\sigma}\equiv\sqrt{m_{\sigma}^{2}-\alpha^{2}}$ . Furthermore, the free-field pole at $J_{*}={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\sigma}={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}(\alpha+i\nu_{\sigma})$ is shifted to

$$ J_{*}=-\frac{3}{2}\left(1+\frac{3}{16\pi^{2}}\frac{g^{2}}{\nu_{\sigma}^{2}m_{\varphi}^{4}}\right)-i\nu_{\sigma}\,. $$

As noted in [35], the heavy field $\sigma$ ’s interaction with the light field $\varphi$ causes it to decay faster than any free field since $\sigma$ ’s coupling to $\varphi$ provides it with another decay channel in addition to the standard dilution due to Hubble expansion. On the other hand, as explained in [34], the enhancement of this decay of $\sigma$ with respect to $m_{\varphi}$ can be interpreted as the washing out of $\sigma$ correlations at long distances due to the enhanced fluctuations of $\varphi$ in the infrared. In this way, we can make contact with in-in perturbation theory wherein the enhanced fluctuations of $\varphi$ are tied to the dynamics of the super-horizon modes [41, 42].

*Figure 2: Plots of the relative error $\rho\equiv|{\mathrm{exact}}-{\mathrm{approx}}|/|{\mathrm{exact}}|$ between the approximation (3.28) to the exact result (A.15), with $\alpha=\frac{1}{2}(3-10^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}8})$ . On the left, we plot this relative error as a function of $J$ , along a line in the complex plane that is parallel to the real axis and passes through the free-field pole $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\sigma}$ , for $m_{\varphi}^{2}=10^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}2}$ and various values of $m_{\sigma}$ . On the right, we plot this relative error at the free-field pole $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\sigma}$ as a function of $m_{\varphi}$ . It can be seen that the relative error between the approximate (3.28) and exact (A.15) results is $\mathcal{O}(m_{\varphi}^{2})$ .*

We have argued that $[G^{\sigma}G^{\varphi}]_{J}$ ’s singularity structure in $J$ can be extracted from the IR contribution $[G^{\sigma}G^{\varphi}]_{J}$ alone since the UV contribution is regular in $J$ . We may then write

$$ [G^{\sigma}G^{\varphi}]_{J}=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\left[\frac{\mathcal{R}(\Delta_{\sigma}+\Delta_{\varphi}+2k)}{J+\Delta_{\sigma}+\Delta_{\varphi}+2k}+\frac{\mathcal{R}(\Delta_{\sigma}+\bar{\Delta}_{\varphi}+2k)}{J+\Delta_{\sigma}+\bar{\Delta}_{\varphi}+2k}+\big(\Delta_{\sigma}\to\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}\big)\right]+f(J)\,, $$

where $\mathcal{R}(J_{*})$ denotes the residue of this function at $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}J_{*}$ and $f(J)$ is an analytic function. Unlike the propagator in §2.3, the bubble does not necessarily decay as $|J|\to\infty$ because the product $G^{\sigma}(\xi)G^{\varphi}(\xi)$ is too singular as $\xi\to 1$ , ultimately leading to the UV divergence $[G^{\sigma}G^{\varphi}]_{J}\sim 1/(8\pi^{2}\epsilon)$ . Analytically continuing our Euclidean results defined on $\xi\in[-1,1]$ to Lorentzian signature with $\xi\in\mathbb{R}$ requires that these coefficients decay sufficiently rapidly to use the Watson-Sommerfeld transform, and we ensure this is possible by adjusting our counterterms, subtracting off the constant—or the $J(J+3)$ terms in cases where the kinetic counterterm is needed—as $J\to\infty$ . Once this is done, the self-energy $\Pi_{\sigma}(J)$ vanishes as $J\to\infty$ and thus we can conclude that $f(J)=0$ . The full self-energy can thus be recovered from the IR contribution $[G^{\sigma}G^{\varphi}]_{J}$ alone. This agrees with (3.28), where we have truncated the sum in (3.32) to only its dominant poles at $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}(\Delta_{\sigma}+\Delta_{\varphi})$ and $-(\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}+\Delta_{\varphi})$ and approximated their residues in the $\Delta_{\varphi}\to 0$ limit.

Before we proceed to study quartic interactions, it will be helpful to understand how the approximation (3.28) arises in Euclidean signature as in [34]. Here, the analysis is much more straightforward since we can use the Euclidean inversion formula (2.12) for non-negative integer $J$ ,

$$ [G^{\sigma}G^{\varphi}]_{J}=\frac{(4\pi)^{\alpha}\Gamma(\alpha)\Gamma(J+1)}{\Gamma(J+2\alpha)}\int_{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}1}^{1}\!\mathrm{d}\xi\,\big(1-\xi^{2}\big)^{\alpha-\frac{1}{2}}C_{J}^{\alpha}(\xi)\,G^{\sigma}(\xi)G^{\varphi}(\xi)\,,{\quad J\in\mathbb{N}\,.} $$

Since this integral is restricted to the finite interval $\xi\in[-1,1]$ , we may expand the light field propagator into its harmonics (2.14),

$$ G^{\varphi}(\xi)=\frac{\Gamma(\alpha+1)}{2\pi^{\alpha+1}}\frac{1}{m_{\varphi}^{2}}+\frac{\Gamma(\alpha)}{2\pi^{\alpha+1}}\sum_{J=1}^{\infty}\frac{J+\alpha}{J(J+2\alpha)+m_{\varphi}^{2}}C_{J}^{\alpha}(\xi)\,, $$

where the first term is the Euclidean zero mode (2.8) propagator. As $m_{\varphi}\to 0$ , we find that the Euclidean zero mode dominates the bubble $[G^{\sigma}G^{\varphi}]_{J}$ , and (3.33) may be approximated as

$$ [G^{\sigma}G^{\varphi}]_{J}\sim\frac{\Gamma(\alpha+1)}{2\pi^{\alpha+1}}\frac{[G^{\sigma}]_{J}}{m_{\varphi}^{2}}\sim\frac{\Gamma(\alpha+1)}{2\pi^{\alpha+1}}\frac{1}{m_{\varphi}^{2}}\frac{1}{(J+\Delta_{\sigma})(J+\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma})}\,. $$

This agrees with (3.28) as $\Delta_{\varphi}\to 0$ when $J\in\mathbb{N}$ , and so we can interpret the enhancement factor as the Euclidean zero mode of the light field $\varphi$ which becomes strongly coupled [38] as $m_{\varphi}\to 0$ .

### 3.2 The Sunset

Having studied the bubble diagram, we now focus on sunset diagrams. Specifically, we will study the theory with interaction and counterterm Lagrangians

$$ \displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{int}}+\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ct}} \displaystyle=\tfrac{1}{2}g\sigma^{2}\varphi^{2}+\tfrac{1}{4!}g_{\varphi}\varphi^{4}+\tfrac{1}{4!}g_{\sigma}\sigma^{4}+\tfrac{1}{2}\delta_{Z_{\sigma}}(\partial\sigma)^{2}+\tfrac{1}{2}\delta_{Z_{\varphi}}(\partial\varphi)^{2}+\tfrac{1}{2}\delta_{m_{\sigma}}\sigma^{2}+\tfrac{1}{2}\delta_{m_{\varphi}}\varphi^{2} \displaystyle+\tfrac{1}{2}\delta_{g}\sigma^{2}\varphi^{2}+\tfrac{1}{4!}\delta_{g_{\varphi}}\varphi^{4}+\tfrac{1}{4!}\delta_{g_{\sigma}}\sigma^{4} $$

and associated interaction vertices

