or equivalently

$$ \Pi_{\varphi}(J)=-g\hskip 0.5pt\hskip 0.5ptG^{\sigma}(1)-\tfrac{1}{2}g_{\varphi}\hskip 0.5ptG^{\varphi}(1)-J(J+2\alpha)\delta_{Z_{\varphi}}-\delta_{m_{\varphi}}\,. $$

For a light field, there is only one pole with maximal real part, and ensuring this pole yields the physically measured mass forces us to choose $\delta_{Z_{\varphi}}=\mathcal{O}(g^{2})$ and $\delta_{m_{\varphi}}=-g\hskip 0.5pt\hskip 0.5ptG^{\sigma}(1)-\tfrac{1}{2}g_{\varphi}\hskip 0.5ptG^{\varphi}(1)+\mathcal{O}(g^{2})$ . Physical corrections to the propagation of both $\sigma$ and $\varphi$ instead occur at $\mathcal{O}(g^{2})$ .

