Given our experience with the bubble diagram in §3.1, we already know which terms in (3.49) will dominate as $\Delta_{\varphi}\to 0$ . The first term, $[G^{\sigma}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\sigma}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\sigma}}]_{J}$ , will contain two factors of $\mathcal{A}(\Delta_{\varphi})\propto\Delta_{\varphi}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}2}$ and have two families of integer-spaced poles at $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}(\Delta_{\sigma}+2\Delta_{\varphi}+2k)$ and $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}(\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}+2\Delta_{\varphi}+2k)$ , for $k\in\mathbb{N}$ .1313 13 From the representation (3.52), it is trivial to see that $[G^{\sigma}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}]_{J}^{\textsc{ir}}$ potentially has poles at $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}(\Delta_{\sigma}+2\Delta_{\varphi}+k)$ and $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}(\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}+2\Delta_{\varphi}+k)$ , for $k\in\mathbb{N}$ . Unfortunately, it is not so easy to see from this form that the residues of the poles vanish for all odd $k$ , and we do not have a proof of this. These residues are trivial to compute—one simply sets $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}(\Delta_{\sigma}+2\Delta_{\varphi}+k)$ and series expands the integrand about $\zeta=0$ to find the coefficient of the $\zeta^{k}$ term. In practice, we have confirmed that residues of the odd $k$ poles vanish up to $k=15$ , though whether or not this is true does not affect our results. Physically, that these odd poles vanish follows from momentum conservation—the pole at $J=-(\Delta_{\sigma}+2\Delta_{\varphi}+k)$ is generated by a long-lived state of a $\Delta_{\sigma}$ “particle” and two $\Delta_{\varphi}$ “particles,” with $k$ units of integer-quantized momentum distributed among them. Since momentum must be conserved, we cannot add momentum along one leg without adding the opposite momentum along the other, and so $k$ must be even to correspond to a long-lived physical state. As $\Delta_{\varphi}\to 0$ , the right-most poles with $k=0$ will encroach on the free-field poles at $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\sigma}$ and $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}$ and dominate the self-energy.

This does not happen with the other two terms in (3.49). By the same logic, $[G^{\sigma}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}\mathcal{G}_{\smash{\bar{\Delta}_{\varphi}}}]_{J}$ will only contain one factor of $\mathcal{A}(\Delta_{\varphi})$ and have two families of poles at $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}(\Delta_{\sigma}+\Delta_{\varphi}+\bar{\Delta}_{\varphi}+2k)={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}(\Delta_{\sigma}+2\alpha+2k)$ and $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}(\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}+2\alpha+2k)$ , neither of which encroach upon the free-field poles as $\Delta_{\varphi}\to 0$ . Similarly, $[G^{\sigma}\mathcal{G}_{\smash{\bar{\Delta}_{\varphi}}}\mathcal{G}_{\smash{\bar{\Delta}_{\varphi}}}]_{J}$ has no factors of $\mathcal{A}(\Delta_{\varphi})$ and poles at $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}(\Delta_{\sigma}+2\bar{\Delta}_{\varphi}+2k)$ and $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}(\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}+2\bar{\Delta}_{\varphi}+2k)$ . Thus, the terms $[G^{\sigma}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}]_{J}$ , $[\mathcal{G}^{\sigma}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}\mathcal{G}_{\smash{\bar{\Delta}_{\varphi}}}]_{J}$ and $[\mathcal{G}^{\sigma}\mathcal{G}_{\smash{\bar{\Delta}_{\varphi}}}\mathcal{G}_{\smash{\bar{\Delta}_{\varphi}}}]_{J}$ will be of order $\Delta_{\varphi}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}3}$ , $\Delta_{\varphi}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}1}$ and $\Delta_{\varphi}^{0}$ , respectively, near the free-field poles as $\Delta_{\varphi}\to 0$ and so we will only need to keep the first. By similar logic, the other sunset diagram $\propto[G^{\sigma}G^{\sigma}G^{\sigma}]_{J}$ will be of $\mathcal{O}(\Delta^{0}_{\varphi})$ as $\Delta_{\varphi}\to 0$ , and so we need not consider it.

The dominant contribution to $\sigma$ ’s self-energy as $\Delta_{\varphi}\to 0$ is then given by

$$ \displaystyle[G^{\sigma}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}]_{J} \displaystyle=\mathcal{N}_{J,\Delta_{\sigma}\hskip 0.5pt2\Delta_{\varphi}}\int_{0}^{\infty}\!\mathrm{d}\zeta\,\zeta^{J+\Delta_{\sigma}+2\Delta_{\varphi}-1}{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$J+\alpha+ \frac{1}{2}, \,\, J+1$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 J + 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right] \displaystyle\qquad\times{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\Delta_{\sigma}, \,\, \Delta_\sigma- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 \Delta_\sigma- 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\Delta_{\varphi}, \,\, \Delta_\varphi- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 \Delta_\varphi- 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]^{2}+(\Delta_{\sigma}\to\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma})\,, $$

where we have introduced the coefficient

$$ \mathcal{N}_{J,\Delta_{\sigma}\hskip 0.5pt2\Delta_{\varphi}}=\frac{2\pi^{\alpha+1}}{4^{J}}\frac{\mathcal{A}(\Delta_{\sigma})\mathcal{A}(\Delta_{\varphi})\mathcal{A}(\Delta_{\varphi})\Gamma(J+1)}{\Gamma(J+\alpha+1)\Gamma(\Delta_{\sigma}+2\Delta_{\varphi})\Gamma(1-\Delta_{\sigma}-2\Delta_{\varphi})}\,\underset{\Delta_{\varphi}\to 0}{\sim}\frac{\Gamma(\alpha)^{2}\mathcal{N}_{J,\Delta_{\sigma}}}{16\pi^{2\alpha+2}\Delta_{\varphi}^{2}}\,. $$

Following the same strategy we used with the bubble diagram, the self-energy near the free-field pole $J=-\Delta_{\sigma}$ is dominated by the IR part of this integrand,

$$ \begin{aligned} {}^{{\textsc{ir}}}_{J}&\approx\frac{\Gamma(\alpha)^{2}}{16\pi^{2\alpha+2}}\frac{\mathcal{N}_{J,\Delta_{\sigma}}}{\Delta_{\varphi}^{2}}\int_{0}^{1}\!\mathrm{d}\zeta\,\zeta^{J+\Delta_{\sigma}+2\Delta_{\varphi}-1}\,{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$J+\alpha+ \frac{1}{2}, \,\, J+1$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 J + 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]\\
&\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\times{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\Delta_{\sigma}, \,\, \Delta_\sigma- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 \Delta_\sigma- 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]+(\Delta_{\sigma}\to\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma})\end{aligned}\,, $$

which is well-approximated by the first term in the series expansion of the integrand about $\zeta=0$ ,

$$ \displaystyle{}^{{\textsc{ir}}}_{J} \displaystyle\approx\left[\frac{\Gamma(\alpha)}{4\pi^{\alpha+1}\Delta_{\varphi}}\right]^{2}\!\frac{1}{(J+\Delta_{\sigma}+2\Delta_{\varphi})(J+\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}+2\Delta_{\varphi})}\,. $$

To this, we must add the UV contribution $[G^{\sigma}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}]_{J}^{\textsc{uv}}$ . This is subleading as $\Delta_{\varphi}\to 0$ , albeit UV divergent. We regularize the general sunset diagram in Appendix C, finding (C.18) as $\alpha\to\frac{3}{2}$ . We find that as $\Delta_{\varphi}\to 0$ , the sunset diagram (3.49) is well-approximated by

