where $\mathcal{U}(\Delta)$ is defined in (C.19). Since $[G^{\sigma}G^{\sigma}G^{\sigma}]_{J}$ is subleading, (3.54) determines the self-energy $\Pi_{\sigma}(J)$ to leading order in $\epsilon$ and $\Delta_{\varphi}$ . Imposing our renormalization conditions, ${\mathrm{Re}}\,\Pi_{\sigma}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\sigma})=0$ and $\Pi^{\prime}_{\sigma}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\sigma})$ , we find second-order corrections to the kinetic and mass counterterms to be

$$ \displaystyle\delta^{\scriptscriptstyle(2)}_{Z_{\sigma}} \displaystyle=-\frac{g^{2}}{(4\pi)^{4}}\left[\frac{1}{\epsilon}-\frac{1}{2\hskip 0.5pt\nu_{\sigma}^{2}\hskip 0.5pt\Delta_{\varphi}^{4}}\right] \displaystyle\delta^{\scriptscriptstyle(2)}_{m_{\sigma}} \displaystyle=-\frac{8g^{2}}{(4\pi)^{4}\epsilon}+\frac{g^{2}}{2(4\pi)^{4}}\frac{m_{\sigma}^{2}}{\nu_{\sigma}^{2}\hskip 0.5pt\Delta_{\varphi}^{4}}+2g^{2}\,\mathcal{U}(\Delta_{\sigma})+4g^{2}\,\mathcal{U}(\Delta_{\varphi}) $$

at leading order in $\Delta_{\varphi}\to 0$ . The imaginary part of the self-energy is unaffected by these real counterterms and is simply

$$ {\mathrm{Im}}\,\Pi_{\sigma}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\sigma})\sim\frac{2g^{2}}{(4\pi)^{4}\nu_{\sigma}\Delta_{\varphi}^{3}}\,,\mathrlap{\qquad\Delta_{\varphi}\to 0\,.} $$

Using (3.8), we find that the free-field pole is shifted by

$$ J_{*}\approx{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\frac{3}{2}\left(1+\frac{9}{128\pi^{4}}\frac{g^{2}}{\nu_{\sigma}^{2}m_{\varphi}^{6}}\right)-i\nu_{\sigma}\,. $$

As with the bubble diagram of §3.1, coupling to the light scalar $\varphi$ causes the heavy scalar $\sigma$ to decay faster than any free field in de Sitter.

This shift of the free-field pole translates into a clear observable in the context of the cosmological collider signal [34]. Ultimately, given any operator which couples to the scalar mode during inflation, it is the leading exponent of this operator in the infrared (i.e. in the late time limit) which fixes the decay of the three-point function $\left\langle\zeta_{{\mathbf{k}}_{1}}\zeta_{{\mathbf{k}}_{3}}\zeta_{{\mathbf{k}}_{3}}\right\rangle$ in the squeezed limit [28]. Therefore, the pole $J_{*}$ contains all the necessary information to characterize the scaling exponents in the shape function. Recalling (1.3), in the interacting theory the decay parameter corresponding to a $\sigma$ exchange in the cosmological collider precisely becomes

$$ \beta=\frac{1}{2}\!\left(1+\frac{27}{128\pi^{4}}\frac{g^{2}}{\nu_{\sigma}^{2}m_{\varphi}^{6}}\right), $$

thereby resulting in a clear suppression of the signal.

