By the same logic we used for (3.49), the first term $[\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}]_{J}$ scales1414 14 From (3.61), we see that even though this term is proportional to $\mathcal{A}(\Delta_{\varphi})^{3}\sim\Delta_{\varphi}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}3}$ , the discontinuity in the inversion formula (2.22) introduces an extra $1/\Gamma(3\Delta_{\varphi})$ that causes this term to scale as $\Delta_{\varphi}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}2}$ as $\Delta_{\varphi}\to 0$ . This still matches with the Euclidean picture—if we replace every internal leg with a zero mode propagator as in (3.34), we find that this diagram should scale as $m_{\varphi}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}6}\propto\Delta_{\varphi}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}3}$ when $J=0$ . At non-zero $J$ , atleast one internal leg must be a non-zero mode, and so this diagram scales as $m_{\varphi}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}4}\propto\Delta_{\varphi}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}2}$ when $J\neq 0$ . as $\Delta_{\varphi}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}2}$ and has poles at $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}(3\Delta_{\varphi}+2k)$ , the second $[\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}\mathcal{G}_{\smash{\bar{\Delta}}_{\varphi}}]_{J}$ scales as $\propto\Delta_{\varphi}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}2}$ and has poles at $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}(2\Delta_{\varphi}+\smash{\bar{\Delta}}_{\varphi}+2k)$ , the third $[\mathcal{G}_{\smash{\bar{\Delta}}_{\varphi}}\mathcal{G}_{\smash{\bar{\Delta}}_{\varphi}}\mathcal{G}_{\smash{\bar{\Delta}}_{\varphi}}]_{J}$ scales as $\propto\Delta_{\varphi}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}1}$ and has poles at $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}(\Delta_{\varphi}+2\bar{\Delta}_{\varphi}+2k)$ , while the last term $[\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}\mathcal{G}_{\smash{\bar{\Delta}}_{\varphi}}\mathcal{G}_{\smash{\bar{\Delta}}_{\varphi}}]_{J}$ scales as $\Delta_{\varphi}^{0}$ and has poles at $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}(3\smash{\bar{\Delta}}_{\varphi}+2k)$ , with $k\in\mathbb{N}$ . We thus see that only the poles of the first term $[\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}]_{J}$ encroach on the free-field pole at $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\varphi}$ and so dominates over the others—it is of order $\Delta_{\varphi}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}3}$ while the others are of order $\Delta_{\varphi}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}2}$ , $\Delta_{\varphi}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}1}$ and $\Delta_{\varphi}^{0}$ , respectively. However, if we are interested in the behavior of the self-energy near the other free-field pole $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\bar{\Delta}_{\varphi}$ , then $[\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}\mathcal{G}_{\smash{\bar{\Delta}_{\varphi}}}]_{J}$ is of order $\Delta_{\varphi}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}3}$ and dominates over the others.

Focusing on $\Pi_{\varphi}(J)$ near $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\varphi}$ , we argued that this is dominated by

$$ [\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}]_{J}=\mathcal{N}_{J,3\Delta_{\varphi}}\int_{0}^{\infty}\!\mathrm{d}\zeta\,\zeta^{J+3\Delta_{\varphi}-1}{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{0.0pt}{1}{J+\alpha+ \frac{1}{2}, \,\, J+1}{2 J + 2 \alpha+ 1}\,\Big|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{0.0pt}{1}{\Delta_{\varphi}, \,\, \Delta_\varphi- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}}{2 \Delta_\varphi- 2 \alpha+ 1}\,\Big|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]^{3} $$

with

$$ \mathcal{N}_{J,3\Delta_{\varphi}}=\frac{2\pi^{\alpha+1}}{4^{J}}\frac{\mathcal{A}(\Delta_{\varphi})^{3}\Gamma(J+1)}{\Gamma(3\Delta_{\varphi})\Gamma(1-3\Delta_{\varphi})\Gamma(J+\alpha+1)}\,. $$

As with (3.50), this integral is dominated by its IR contribution near $J=-\Delta_{\varphi}$ , and we may approximate it by the closest pole,1515 15 When $J=0$ , we can easily evaluate (3.59) by decomposing the propagator into its zero mode and non-zero mode contributions, cf. (3.34). Using the definition of $[G^{\varphi}G^{\varphi}G^{\varphi}]_{J}$ given in (2.11), we have that (3.59) reduces to the product of three zero mode propagators multiplied the inverse square of the zero mode profiles (2.8).

$$ [\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}]_{J}^{\textsc{ir}}\approx\frac{3}{2\alpha}\left[\frac{\Gamma(\alpha)}{4\pi^{\alpha+1}\Delta_{\varphi}}\right]^{2}\frac{1}{J+3\Delta_{\varphi}}\,. $$

