near $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\varphi}$ . Since this diagram also dominates over the other, $\varphi$ ’s self-energy is well-approximated by

$$ \Pi_{\varphi}(J)\approx\frac{g_{\varphi}^{2}}{3!}\left[\frac{1}{64\pi^{4}\Delta_{\varphi}^{2}}\frac{1}{J+3\Delta_{\varphi}}-\frac{J(J+3)}{2(4\pi)^{4}\epsilon}-\frac{1}{64\pi^{4}\epsilon}+3\,\mathcal{U}(\Delta_{\varphi})\right]-J(J+3)\hskip 0.5pt\delta^{\scriptscriptstyle(2)}_{Z_{\varphi}}-\delta_{m_{\varphi}}^{\scriptscriptstyle(2)}\,. $$

This self-energy is completely real, and so its effect on the free-field pole $J=-\Delta_{\varphi}$ can be completely absorbed by the counterterms. Requiring ${\mathrm{Re}}\,\Pi({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\varphi})=\Pi^{\prime}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\varphi})=0$ , we find that

$$ \displaystyle\delta_{Z_{\varphi}} \displaystyle\sim-\frac{g_{\varphi}^{2}}{12(4\pi)^{4}\epsilon}-\frac{g_{\varphi}^{2}}{18(4\pi)^{4}\Delta_{\varphi}^{4}} \displaystyle\delta_{m_{\varphi}} \displaystyle\sim-\frac{2g_{\varphi}^{2}}{3(4\pi)^{4}\epsilon}+\frac{g_{\varphi}^{2}}{6(4\pi)^{4}\Delta_{\varphi}^{3}}+\frac{1}{2}g_{\varphi}^{2}\,\mathcal{U}(\Delta_{\varphi})\,, $$

as $\Delta_{\varphi}\to 0$ . Of course, this is not to say that quantum fluctuations do not change the long-distance behavior of $\langle\varphi(x)\varphi(y)\rangle$ , as the self-energy

$$ \Pi_{\varphi}(J)\sim\frac{g_{\varphi}^{2}}{3(4\pi)^{4}\Delta_{\varphi}^{3}}\left[\frac{2\Delta_{\varphi}}{J+3\Delta_{\varphi}}+\frac{J(J+3)}{6\Delta_{\varphi}}-\frac{1}{2}\right],\quad\Delta_{\varphi}\to 0\,, $$

is still non-trivial in this limit.

