## Appendix A Formulary

In this appendix, we collect various definitions and conventions of the special functions we use in the main text as well as several useful formulas.

Many of our expressions will contain large ratios and products of gamma functions, and so we adopt the common shorthand

$$ \Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$a, b, c, \cdots$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$d, e, f, \cdots$}}\right]=\frac{\Gamma(a)\Gamma(b)\Gamma(c)\cdots}{\Gamma(d)\Gamma(e)\Gamma(f)\cdots}\quad\text{and}\quad\Gamma[a,b,c,\cdots]=\Gamma(a)\Gamma(b)\Gamma(c)\cdots\,, $$

though we will also use actual products and ratios when they look better. For arguments with large imaginary parts, the magnitude of the gamma function is exponentially suppressed,

$$ \big|\Gamma(x+iy)\big|=\sqrt{2\pi}\hskip 0.5pt\hskip 0.5pt|y|^{x-\frac{1}{2}}\hskip 0.5pt\hskip 0.5pt{{\mathrm{e}}}^{-\pi|y|/2}\,,\mathrlap{\qquad y\to\pm\infty\,,} $$

with both $x$ and $y$ real. The digamma function $\psi(z)$ is defined as $\psi(z)=\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}z}\log\Gamma(z)$ and usefully obeys the functional equation $\psi(z+1)=\psi(z)+1/z$ . Furthermore, ${\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\psi(1)=\gamma_{\textsc{e}}\approx{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}0.577216$ is the Euler-Mascheroni constant.

The Gaussian or ordinary hypergeometric function is defined in the disc $|z|<1$ by the series

$$ {}_{2}F_{1}(a,b;c;z)\equiv{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$a, \,\, b$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$c$}}\,\bigg|\,z\,\right]=\frac{\Gamma(c)}{\Gamma(a)\Gamma(b)}\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{\Gamma(a+n)\Gamma(b+n)}{\Gamma(c+n)}\frac{z^{n}}{n!}\,. $$

This can also be defined through the Mellin-Barnes integral [65]

$$ {}_{2}F_{1}(a,b,c;z)=\frac{\Gamma(c)}{\Gamma(a)\Gamma(b)}\int_{\gamma}\frac{\mathrm{d}s}{2\pi i}\frac{\Gamma(s)\Gamma(a-s)\Gamma(b-s)}{\Gamma(c-s)}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}z)^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}s}\,, $$

in which the contour $\gamma$ runs along the imaginary $s$ -axis and separates the so-called “left poles” generated by $\Gamma(s)$ from the “right poles” generated by $\Gamma(a-s)\Gamma(b-s)$ .1616 16 This is typically written with $s\to{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}s$ , but this form it will be slightly more convenient for our purposes. For certain values of $a$ , $b$ , and $c$ , the contour $\gamma$ may need to be deformed to separate these families of poles and may not always be strictly parallel to the imaginary axis, as illustrated in Figure 3. This integral representation converges as long as $z$ is not on the positive real axis, $z\notin[0,\infty)$ .

Any function of two points on the Euclidean sphere that is invariant under its isometries must be a function of the embedding distance $\xi\in[{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1,1]$ , and so admits a decomposition into any complete set of orthogonal polynomials on the interval. An especially useful set for our purposes are the Gegenbauer polynomials. These can be defined in terms of the Gegenbauer- $C$ functions [66], which are solutions to the differential equations

$$ \left[(\xi^{2}-1)\frac{\mathrm{d}^{2}}{\mathrm{d}\xi^{2}}+(2J+1)\,\xi\,\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\xi}-J(J+2\alpha)\right]C_{J}^{\alpha}(\xi)=0\,, $$

and can be expressed in terms of the hypergeometric function as

$$ C_{J}^{\alpha}(\xi)\equiv\frac{\Gamma(J+2\alpha)}{\Gamma(J+1)\Gamma(2\alpha)}\,{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\minus J, \,\, J+2 \alpha$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$\alpha+ \tfrac{1}{2}$}}\,\bigg|\,\frac{1-\xi}{2}\,\right]\,. $$

The Gegenbauer polynomials are then defined as the Gegenbauer- $C$ functions restricted to non-negative integer $J$ . Furthermore, for large argument, these behave as

$$ C_{J}^{\alpha}(\xi)\sim\Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$J+\alpha$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$\alpha\,,\,J+1$}}\right](2\hskip 0.5pt\xi)^{J}+\Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$J+2 \alpha\,,\, -(J+\alpha)$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$\alpha\,,\,\minus J\,,\, J+1$}}\right](2\xi)^{-(J+2\alpha)}\,, $$

as $|\xi|\to\infty$ . In particular, at the endpoints of the interval

$$ C_{J}^{\alpha}(1)=\frac{\Gamma(J+2\alpha)}{\Gamma(J+1)\Gamma(2\alpha)}\qquad\text{and}\qquad C_{J}^{\alpha}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1)=\frac{\cos\pi(J+\alpha)}{\cos\pi\alpha}\frac{\Gamma(J+2\alpha)}{\Gamma(J+1)\Gamma(2\alpha)}\,, $$

and so both grow as $J^{2\alpha-1}$ for large integer $J$ . However, $C_{J}^{\alpha}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1)$ grows exponentially as ${\mathrm{Im}}\,J\to\pm\infty$ . The Gegenbauer $C$ -functions are thus very poorly behaved for non-integer $J$ .

Any smooth function $H(\xi)$ can then be decomposed as

$$ H(\xi)=\sum_{J=0}^{\infty}(J+\alpha)[H]_{J}^{\phantom{\alpha}}\,C_{J}^{\alpha}(\xi)\,, $$

where the coefficients $[H]_{J}$ can be extracted via

$$ [H]_{J}=\frac{(4\pi)^{\alpha}\Gamma(\alpha)\Gamma(J+1)}{\Gamma(J+2\alpha)}\int_{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}1}^{1}\!\mathrm{d}\xi\,\big(1-\xi^{2}\big)^{\alpha-\frac{1}{2}}C_{J}^{\alpha}(\xi)H(\xi)\, $$

for integer $J$ . As discussed in the main text, they may also be extracted via a contour integral over the Gegenbauer $Q$ -function

$$ [H]_{J}=\frac{(4\pi)^{\alpha}\Gamma(\alpha)\Gamma(J+1)}{\Gamma(J+2\alpha)}\oint_{\mathcal{C}}\frac{\mathrm{d}\xi}{2\pi i}\big(\xi^{2}-1\big)^{\alpha-\frac{1}{2}}Q_{J}^{\alpha}(\xi)H(\xi)\,, $$

where $\mathcal{C}$ wraps the interval $\xi\in[-1,1]$ in a counter-clockwise fashion. The Gegenbauer- $Q$ are the other solution to (A.5) and may be defined as

$$ Q_{J}^{\alpha}(\xi)\equiv\frac{2^{1-J-2\alpha}\pi\Gamma(J+2\alpha)}{\Gamma(\alpha)\Gamma(J+\alpha+1)}(\xi-1)^{-J-2\alpha}{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$J+\alpha+ \frac{1}{2}, \,\, J+2 \alpha$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 J + 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,\frac{2}{1-\xi}\,\right]\,. $$

Importantly, $Q_{J}^{\alpha}(\xi)$ is the unique solution to (A.5) which decays as $\xi^{-J-2\alpha}$ as $|\xi|\to\infty$ . This implies that, as long as the integral (A.11) converges, it defines the appropriate analytic continuation of $[H]_{J}$ to non-integer $J$ that is well-behaved in the right-half $J$ -plane.

The functions we work with will have a discontinuity along $\xi\in[1,\infty)$ , and so we may write

$$ [H]_{J}=-\frac{2\pi^{\alpha+1}\Gamma(J+1)}{4^{J}\hskip 0.5pt\Gamma(J+\alpha+1)}\int_{0}^{\infty}\!\frac{\mathrm{d}\zeta}{2\pi i}\,\zeta^{J-1}\,{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$J+\alpha+ \frac{1}{2}, \,\, J+1$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 J + 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]\,{\mathrm{disc}}\,H(\zeta)\,, $$

where $\zeta\equiv 2/(\xi-1)$ . The discontinuity

$$ {\mathrm{disc}}\,({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1/\zeta)^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}\Delta}={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}2i\zeta^{\Delta}\sin\pi\Delta=-\frac{2\pi i\hskip 0.5pt\zeta^{\Delta}}{\Gamma(\Delta)\Gamma(1-\Delta)}\,,\mathrlap{\quad\zeta>0\,,} $$

will be particularly useful throughout the text.

In our notation, the inversion $[G^{\sigma}G^{\varphi}]_{J}$ of the bubble diagram was found in [35] to be

$$ \displaystyle[G^{\sigma}G^{\varphi}]_{J}=\frac{\Gamma(\Delta_{\varphi})}{16\pi^{\alpha}}\frac{\cos\pi\Delta_{\varphi}}{\sin\pi(\alpha-\Delta_{\varphi})}\Gamma\big[2-2\alpha\,,\,J+1\,,\,\tfrac{1}{2}(J+1-\alpha+\Delta_{\varphi})\big]\times \displaystyle\qquad\qquad\quad\Gamma\big[J+2-\alpha+\Delta_{\varphi}\,,\,\tfrac{1}{2}(J+2\alpha+\Delta_{\varphi}-\Delta_{\sigma})\,,\,\tfrac{1}{2}(J+\Delta_{\varphi}+\Delta_{\sigma})\big]\times \displaystyle{}_{7}\!\hskip 0.5pt\hskip 0.5pt\hskip 0.5pt\hbox to0.0pt{\hskip 1.52777pt\hbox{$\overline{\hbox{}}$}\hss}{\hbox{$V$}}_{\!6}\Big[J+1-\alpha+\Delta_{\varphi}\,;\,1-\alpha\,,\,1-2\alpha+\Delta_{\varphi}\,,\,J+1\,,\,\tfrac{1}{2}(J+2\alpha+\Delta_{\varphi}-\Delta_{\sigma})\,,\,\tfrac{1}{2}(J+\Delta_{\varphi}+\Delta_{\sigma})\Big] \displaystyle\qquad\qquad\quad+(\Delta_{\varphi},\Delta_{\sigma})\to(\bar{\Delta}_{\varphi},\Delta_{\sigma})+(\Delta_{\varphi},\Delta_{\sigma})\to(\Delta_{\sigma},\Delta_{\varphi})+(\Delta_{\varphi},\Delta_{\sigma})\to(\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma},\Delta_{\varphi}) $$

where

$$ {}_{7}\!\hskip 0.5pt\hskip 0.5pt\hskip 0.5pt\hbox to0.0pt{\hskip 1.52777pt\hbox{$\overline{\hbox{}}$}\hss}{\hbox{$V$}}_{\!6}[a;b,c,d,e,f]=\frac{{}_{7}V_{6}[a;b,c,d,e,f]}{\Gamma\big[\frac{1}{2}a,1+a-b,1+a-c,1+a-d,1+a-e,1+a-f\big]} $$

is a regularized ${}_{7}V_{6}$ , the so-called very well-poised ${}_{7}F_{6}$ hypergeometric function [65],

$$ {}_{7}V_{6}[a;b,c,d,e,f]={}_{7}F_{6}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$a\,,\, 1+\frac{1}{2}a\,,\, b \,,\, c \,,\, d\,,\, e\,,\, f$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$\frac{1}{2}a\,,\, 1+a - b\,,\,1+a-c\,,\,1+a-d\,,\,1+a-e\,,\,1+a-f $}}\,\bigg|\,1\,\right]\,, $$

with

$$ {}_{p}F_{q}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$a_1\,,\, a_2\,, \cdots\,,\, a_p$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$b_1\,,\, b_2\,,\, \cdots\,,\, b_q$}}\,\bigg|\,z\,\right]=\Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$b_1 \,,\, b_2\,,\, \cdots\,,\, b_q$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$a_1\,,\, a_2 \,,\, \cdots\,,\, a_p$}}\right]\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\,\Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$a_1 + n\,,\, a_2 + n\,,\, \cdots\,,\, a_p + n$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$b_1 + n \,,\, b_2 + n\,,\, \cdots\,,\, b_q + n$}}\right]\frac{z^{n}}{n!} $$

for $|z|<1$ is the generalized hypergeometric function. Such regularized hypergeometric functions are entire in all of their parameters, while a hypergeometric function is said to be well-poised if $p=q+1$ and $1+a_{1}=b_{1}+a_{2}=b_{2}+a_{3}=\cdots=b_{q}+a_{q+1}$ . Such functions are very well-poised if they are well-poised and $a_{2}=1+\frac{1}{2}a_{1}$ .

## Appendix B Uniqueness of the Interpolation

In the main text, we defined Lorentzian de Sitter two-point functions on $\xi\in\mathbb{R}$ via the analytic continuation of their Euclidean counterparts defined on $\xi\in[{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1,1]$ . These Euclidean correlation functions were expressed as sums over integer momenta $J$ of weighted Gegenbauer polynomials, which only converge1717 17 Strictly speaking, $\alpha$ also needs to be small enough for these sums to converge, as the propagator is too singular when $\alpha\geq\frac{3}{2}$ to be faithfully represented by a Gegenbauer polynomial interpolation. when $|\xi|<1$ . To analytically continue these expressions to $|\xi|\geq 1$ , we rely on the Watson-Sommerfeld transformation to convert the discrete sum into a contour integral over the product of a meromorphic “interpolation” of the summand and a “kernel” with unit residue poles at non-negative integers. As we have discussed, there are infinitely many interpolations that match the summand at the integers, but Carlson’s theorem guarantees that there can be only one with sub-exponential growth as $|J|\to\infty$ . In this appendix, we give a pedagogical explanation for why this well-behaved interpolation, given by the Lorentzian inversion formula (2.22), is the correct one to use for analytically continuing two-point functions to $\xi\in\mathbb{R}$ .

We can illustrate this simply by applying the Watson-Sommerfeld transformation to (A.3) to derive the Mellin representation (A.4) for ${}_{2}F_{1}(a,b\hskip 0.5pt;\hskip 0.5ptc\hskip 0.5pt;\hskip 0.5ptz)$ . The most obvious interpolation is

$$ \frac{\Gamma(a+n)\Gamma(b+n)}{\Gamma(c+n)}\frac{z^{n}}{n!}\to\frac{\Gamma(a+s)\Gamma(b+s)}{\Gamma(c+s)\Gamma(s+1)}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}z)^{s}{{\mathrm{e}}}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}i\pi s}\,, $$

and so, with the kernel $k(s)={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}{{\mathrm{e}}}^{i\pi s}\Gamma({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}s)\Gamma(s+1)$ , we can rewrite the sum as

$$ {}_{2}F_{1}(a,b\hskip 0.5pt;\hskip 0.5ptc\hskip 0.5pt;\hskip 0.5ptz)=-\frac{\Gamma(c)}{\Gamma(a)\Gamma(b)}\int_{\mathcal{C}}\frac{\mathrm{d}s}{2\pi i}\frac{\Gamma(a+s)\Gamma(b+s)\Gamma({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}s)}{\Gamma(c+s)}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}z)^{s} $$

where the contour $\mathcal{C}$ sandwiches the positive real axis, coming from $s=\infty+i\epsilon$ to $s=i\epsilon$ , enclosing the pole at $s=0$ , and then going off to $s=\infty-i\epsilon$ .

Unfortunately, the integral in (B.2) still is only absolutely convergent for $|z|<1$ , and so nothing has yet been accomplished. To analytically continue to $|z|\geq 1$ , we must deform $\mathcal{C}$ into a contour along which the integral decays more rapidly as $|s|\to\infty$ . The main factor to pay attention to is

$$ |({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}z)^{s}|=\rho^{k}{{\mathrm{e}}}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}\vartheta t}\,, $$

where we have written ${\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}z=\rho\hskip 0.5pt\hskip 0.5pt{{\mathrm{e}}}^{i\vartheta}$ and $s=k+it$ . This tells us that the integrand is additionally exponentially suppressed if we approach the point $s=\infty$ in the upper (lower) half-plane when $\vartheta>0$ ( $\vartheta<0$ ). Ideally, we could take advantage of this exponential suppression and arrive at an absolutely convergent integral representation for ${}_{2}F_{1}(a,b;c;z)$ by deforming $\mathcal{C}$ into a contour $\gamma$ that lies parallel to the imaginary axis. However, this would also require that the interpolation decays quickly enough in the opposite direction—the lower or upper half-plane when $\vartheta>0$ or $\vartheta<0$ , respectively—that we can ignore the resulting arcs at infinity.

Using (A.2), the integrand behaves as

$$ \left|\frac{\Gamma(a+s)\Gamma(b+s)\Gamma({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}s)}{\Gamma(c+s)}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}z)^{s}\right|\sim 2\pi\rho^{k}\,t^{{\mathrm{Re}}(a+b-c)-1}{{\mathrm{e}}}^{-t\vartheta-\pi|t|}\,,\mathrlap{\quad|t|\to\infty\,,} $$

we see that the integrand is always exponentially suppressed as $t\to\pm\infty$ as long as $\vartheta\neq{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\pi$ . This is where Carlson’s theorem comes in: any entire function that vanishes on the positive integers and grows slower than ${{\mathrm{e}}}^{\pi|s|}$ as $|s|\to\infty$ must be identically zero. So, if we instead modify the interpolation by adding to it an arbitrary regular function $f(s)$ multiplied by $\sin\pi s$ , designed to vanish at all positive integers, this ruins the interpolation’s asymptotic behavior as $|s|\to\infty$ and it instead grows exponentially at least as fast as ${{\mathrm{e}}}^{\pi|s|}$ as $|s|\to\infty$ . This interpolation is thus useless for defining an analytic continuation for $|z|\geq 1$ whereas (B.1), unique in its asymptotic behavior, is the one that must be used to go beyond the original domain of convergence $|z|<1$ . An analogous discussion applies to the Gegenbauer polynomial expansions like (2.17), since asymptotically (A.7) they have the same polynomial dependence on their argument.

## Appendix C UV Divergences of de Sitter Self-Energies

Both the bubble and sunset diagrams are UV divergent and, in this appendix, we regularize them via dimensional regularization. Specifically, we first evaluate these diagrams at an $\alpha=(3-\epsilon)/2$ for which they converge. For the bubble and sunset diagrams, this naively requires that ${\mathrm{Re}}\,\alpha<1$ and ${\mathrm{Re}}\,\alpha<\frac{5}{6}$ , respectively. We then analytically continue these results to $\alpha=\frac{3}{2}$ and $\epsilon\to 0$ , keeping the masses $m_{\varphi}$ and $m_{\sigma}$ fixed. That they diverge when $\alpha=\frac{3}{2}$ is represented by a set of poles in $\epsilon$ , which we then subtract with our mass and kinetic counterterms.

Unfortunately, while we can efficiently extract the self-energies’ singularities in $J$ from the representations derived in the main text (3.19) and (3.52), there is a sort of conservation of trouble and it is quite difficult to extract their $\epsilon\to 0$ behavior for $\alpha=\frac{3}{2}$ , or in any even spacetime dimension with $D>2$ . To see the essence of this problem, we can consider the free field propagator in the form

$$ \begin{aligned} G(\zeta)&=\frac{\Gamma\big(\alpha-\tfrac{1}{2}\big)}{(4\pi)^{\alpha+\frac{1}{2}}}\left(-\frac{1}{\zeta}\right)^{\frac{1}{2}-\alpha}\!\!{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\frac{1}{2} + \alpha- \Delta, \,\, \frac{1}{2} - \alpha+ \Delta$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$\frac{3}{2}- \alpha$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\frac{1}{\zeta}\,\right]\\
&+\frac{\Gamma(\Delta)}{(4\pi)^{\alpha+\frac{1}{2}}}\,\Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\frac{1}{2} - \alpha\,,\,2 \alpha- \Delta$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$\frac{1}{2} + \alpha- \Delta\,,\, \frac{1}{2} - \alpha+ \Delta$}}\right]\hskip 0.5pt\hskip 0.5pt{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\Delta, \,\, 2 \alpha- \Delta$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$\alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\frac{1}{\zeta}\,\right]\,\end{aligned}\,, $$

which is useful for constructing a series expansion in the deep UV, $\zeta\to\infty$ . Unfortunately, both terms in (C.1) are individually singular as $\alpha\to\frac{3}{2}$ , even though their sum is well-defined. These cancellations are necessary in order for the series expansion of (C.1) to the logarithms of $\zeta$ that appear in (C.1) when $\alpha=\frac{3}{2}$ . To make matters worse, these cancellations occur between terms at different orders in the series expansion around $\zeta=\infty$ , which makes it exceedingly difficult to track which terms in (C.1) dominate the bubble and sunset diagrams as $\epsilon\to 0$ , and to efficiently extract their divergences.

Instead, we will rely on the Mellin representation of these self-energies to extract their UV behavior. Such representations have found wide use in the study of flat space Feynman diagrams—for instance, see [67] for a pedagogical review—but also the study of de Sitter correlation functions in the flat slicing [68, 69, 70, 71], though it will be helpful to also review them here in the context of Euclidean de Sitter correlation functions. We will first illustrate these techniques using the propagator (2.27) and the bubble diagram, where we have exact results to which we can compare. Finally, we apply them to the sunset diagram.

### C.1 The Propagator

Let us consider one-half of the propagator (2.27)

$$ \displaystyle[\mathcal{G}_{\Delta}]_{J}=\mathcal{N}_{J,\Delta}\int_{0}^{\infty}\!\mathrm{d}\zeta\,\zeta^{J+\Delta-1}\,{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$J+\alpha+ \frac{1}{2}, \,\, J+1$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2J + 2 \alpha+1 $}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\Delta, \,\, \Delta- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 \Delta- 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right] $$

with the coefficient $\mathcal{N}_{J,\Delta}$ given by (2.28) and the full propagator $[G]_{J}=[\mathcal{G}_{\Delta}]_{J}+[\mathcal{G}_{\bar{\Delta}}]_{J}$ given by this contribution plus its shadow $\bar{\Delta}=2\alpha-\Delta$ . This integral can be evaluated exactly and is singular when $\alpha=(3-\epsilon)/2\to\frac{3}{2}$ , with pole

$$ [\mathcal{G}_{\Delta}]_{J}\sim-\frac{\tan\pi\Delta}{\pi(J+1)(J+2)}\frac{1}{\epsilon}\,,\mathrlap{\qquad\epsilon\to 0\,.} $$

Of course, $[G]_{J}$ is regular as $\epsilon\to 0$ , and (C.3) cancels against an equal and opposite contribution from its shadow $[\mathcal{G}_{\bar{\Delta}}]_{J}$ .

*Figure 3: Mellin-Barnes integration contours for the $\mathcal{G}_{\Delta}(\zeta)$ for a light [left] and heavy [right] fields. For light fields, the original integration contour $\gamma^{\prime}$ must snake around the “left” pole at $s=\alpha-\frac{1}{2}-\Delta$ in order to completely separate the left and right poles. We will deform this contour to one that runs parallel to the imaginary $s$ -axis, denoted $\gamma$ , by including the contribution from this pole. This is unnecessary for both heavy fields and the Mellin-Barnes representation of $\mathcal{G}_{\bar{\Delta}}$ .*

Our basic strategy is to replace the hypergeometric function from the propagator in (C.2) with its Mellin representation (A.4), which we write as

$$ \displaystyle{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\Delta, \,\, \Delta- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 \Delta- 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]= \displaystyle\Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$2 \Delta- 2 \alpha+ 1$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$\Delta\,,\, \Delta- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}\right]\int_{\gamma}\frac{\mathrm{d}s}{2\pi i}\,\Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\Delta- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2} + s\,,\, \minus s\,, \, \alpha- \frac{1}{2} - s$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$\Delta- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2} - s$}}\right]\zeta^{\hskip 0.5pt\alpha-\Delta-\frac{1}{2}-s}\,. $$

We can then integrate over $\zeta$ . As pictured in Figure 3, the integrand of (C.4) has a set of “left” poles at $s=\alpha-\frac{1}{2}-\Delta-k$ and two sets of “right” poles at $s=k$ and $s=\alpha-\frac{1}{2}+k$ , with $k\in\mathbb{N}$ a non-negative integer. The contour $\gamma$ is chosen such that it separates these left and right poles. For a heavy field, this means that the contour $\gamma$ can be chosen to run parallel to the imaginary axis in the strip ${\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\frac{1}{2}<{\mathrm{Re}}\,s<0$ , from ${\mathrm{Im}}\,s={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\infty$ to ${\mathrm{Im}}\,s=+\infty$ . The same is true for light fields with dimensions $\alpha-\frac{1}{2}<\Delta<\alpha$ , though here the strip is reduced to $\alpha-\frac{1}{2}-\Delta<{\mathrm{Re}}\,s<0$ . For light fields with dimensions below $\Delta<\alpha-\frac{1}{2}$ , the right-most left pole is actually to the right of the left-most right pole, and so the contour can no longer be purely parallel to the imaginary axis and separate the left and right poles. However, we can deform this contour to be parallel to the imaginary axis and lie in the strip $-\frac{3}{2}+\sqrt{\alpha^{2}-m^{2}}<{\mathrm{Re}}\,s<0$ , at the cost of including the residue of the pole at $s=\alpha-\frac{1}{2}-\Delta$ , which is thankfully just $1$ .1818 18 We will not consider “conformally-coupled” scalar fields with $m^{2}=\alpha^{2}-\frac{1}{4}$ or equivalently dimension $\Delta=\alpha-\frac{1}{2}$ . In this case, the left-most right pole and right-most left pole overlap, and the Mellin representation is not so useful. However, in this case, the propagator takes such a simple form $G(\zeta)=({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1/\zeta)^{\frac{1}{2}-\alpha}\Gamma\big(\alpha-\frac{1}{2}\big)/(4\pi)^{\alpha+\frac{1}{2}}$ that the inversion formula (2.22) can be done exactly, and so this more complicated analysis is unnecessary. The upside is that we will always choose the contour $\gamma$ to be parallel to the ${\mathrm{Im}}\,s$ axis, with a small negative real part.

With (C.4) inserted (C.2), we can perform the integral over $\zeta$ to find

$$ [\mathcal{G}_{\Delta}]_{J}=\int_{\gamma}\frac{\mathrm{d}s}{2\pi i}\,\Gamma\big(\tfrac{3}{2}-\alpha+s\big)\Gamma({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}s)\mathcal{F}(s) $$

with

$$ \mathcal{F}(s)=\frac{\sin\pi\Delta}{2\pi\sin\pi(\alpha-\Delta)}\,\Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$ 1 + s\,,\, \alpha- \frac{1}{2} - s\,,\, \Delta- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2} + s\,,\, J + \alpha- \frac{1}{2} - s$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$J + \alpha+ \frac{3}{2} + s\,,\, \Delta- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2} - s$}}\right]\,. $$

The integral over $\zeta$ converges when ${\mathrm{Re}}\,s>\alpha-\frac{3}{2}$ and ${\mathrm{Re}}\,(J-s+\alpha)>1$ , which is satisfied by the contour $\gamma$ as long as $\alpha<\frac{3}{2}$ , or for $\epsilon>0$ .1919 19 We will assume throughout this analysis that ${\mathrm{Re}}\,J$ is always large enough to avoid any potential $J$ singularities. Relaxing this assumption would not change our conclusions but only make the analysis more tedious, and it is easier to extract the singularity structure in $J$ using the techniques presented in the main text.

*Figure 4: A singularity may develop in (C.5) if the contour $\gamma$ is “pinched” between left and right poles as $\epsilon\to 0$ [left]. We can compute the resulting singularity by moving the contour $\gamma$ past the pinching pole and picking up its residue [right]. The resulting integral is regular as $\epsilon\to 0$ , so the $\epsilon$ singularity is fully contained within this residue and may be easily calculated.*

The integral (C.5) is well-defined and thus non-singular as long as the contour $\gamma$ separates the left poles from the right poles. The only way a singularity can develop is if the contour $\gamma$ gets “pinched” by the left-most right pole at $s=0$ and the right-most left pole at $s=\alpha-\frac{3}{2}={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\epsilon/2$ , as illustrated in Figure 4. As $\epsilon\to 0$ , we can extract this singularity by deforming the contour past it, at the price of picking up its residue,

$$ [\mathcal{G}_{\Delta}]_{J}=\underset{{s=-\epsilon/2}}{{\mathrm{res}}}\left[\Gamma\big(\tfrac{\epsilon}{2}-s\big)\Gamma({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}s)\mathcal{F}(s)\right]+\int_{\gamma}\frac{\mathrm{d}s}{2\pi i}\,\Gamma^{/\{0\}}\big(\tfrac{\epsilon}{2}-s\big)\Gamma({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}s)\mathcal{F}(s)\,, $$

where we use $\Gamma^{/\{0\}}(z)$ to denote that the contour $\gamma$ is deformed to avoid the $z=0$ singularity of $\Gamma(z)$ . Similarly, we will use $\Gamma^{/\{0,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1,\ldots\}}(z)$ to denote that the contour $\gamma$ avoids the $z=0,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1,\ldots$ singularities of $\Gamma(z)$ . The remaining integral in (C.7) is completely regular as $\epsilon\to 0$ and so the only divergence comes from the residue in (C.7),

$$ [\mathcal{G}_{\Delta}]_{J}\sim-\frac{\sec\pi\alpha\sin\pi\Delta}{2\sin\pi(\alpha-\Delta)}\,\Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$J+1\,,\, \Delta- 1$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$J+2 \alpha\,,\, \Delta- 2 \alpha+2 $}}\right]\sim-\frac{\tan\pi\Delta}{\pi(J+1)(J+2)}\frac{1}{\epsilon}\,, $$

as $\epsilon\to 0$ , which recovers the UV divergence (C.3) from the exact result.

### C.2 The Bubble

A similar strategy works for the bubble diagram, though it will be more convenient to sum over the dimensions and their shadows at the outset. For instance, using (C.4) in (3.17), we have

$$ \begin{aligned} {}_{J}=\int\!\hskip 0.5pt\frac{\mathrm{d}s_{1}}{2\pi i}\frac{\mathrm{d}s_{2}}{2\pi i}&\,\,\Gamma\big(2-2\alpha+s_{1}+s_{2}\big)\Gamma\big(\tfrac{3}{2}-\alpha+s_{1}+s_{2}\big)\\
\times&\,\,\Gamma\big[{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}s_{1}\,,\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}s_{2}\,,\,\alpha-\tfrac{1}{2}-s_{1}\,,\,\alpha-\tfrac{1}{2}-s_{2}\big]\mathcal{F}(s_{1},s_{2})\end{aligned}\,, $$

where the $s_{1}$ and $s_{2}$ contours (which we denote $\gamma_{1}$ and $\gamma_{2}$ , respectively, but will suppress in our expressions going forward) are chosen to run parallel to the imaginary $s_{1}$ and $s_{2}$ axes, with small negative real parts, as described in the previous section. Here, we have also defined the function

$$ \begin{aligned} \mathcal{F}(s_{1},s_{2})&=\frac{(4\pi)^{-\alpha-\frac{3}{2}}\sin\pi(\Delta_{\sigma}+\Delta_{\varphi})}{\sin\pi(\alpha-\Delta_{\sigma})\sin\pi(\alpha-\Delta_{\varphi})}\frac{\Gamma(J+2\alpha-1-s_{1}-s_{2})}{\Gamma(J+2+s_{1}+s_{2})}\\
&\qquad\qquad\times\Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{0.0pt}{0}{\Delta_\varphi- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2} + s_1\,,\, \Delta_\sigma- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2} + s_2}{\Delta_\varphi-\alpha+ \frac{1}{2} - s_1\,,\, \Delta_\sigma-\alpha+ \frac{1}{2} -s_2}\right]+\cdots\end{aligned}\,, $$

where the $\cdots$ denote the three other permutations under $\Delta_{\sigma}\to\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}=2\alpha-\Delta_{\sigma}$ and $\Delta_{\varphi}\to\bar{\Delta}_{\varphi}=2\alpha-\Delta_{\varphi}$ . This Mellin representation is valid (for large enough ${\mathrm{Re}}\,J$ ) as long as $\alpha$ is such that the contours can sit to the left of the right poles generated by $\Gamma({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}s_{1})$ and $\Gamma({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}s_{2})$ , and to the right of the left poles generated by $\Gamma(2-2\alpha+s_{1}+s_{2})$ and $\Gamma\big(\tfrac{3}{2}-\alpha+s_{1}+s_{2}\big)$ . This is possible as long as $\alpha<1$ , and we will need to analytically continue this result from $\alpha<1$ to $\alpha\to\frac{3}{2}$ to extract the bubble’s UV divergences.

*Figure 5: As $\alpha\to 1$ [left], the right-most left pole [green] traps the integration contour, here shown as a crossed circle in [yellow], between the right poles [red/blue]. These poles form lines in the ${\mathrm{Re}}\,s_{1}$ – ${\mathrm{Re}}\,s_{2}$ plane, with ${\mathrm{Im}}\,s_{1}={\mathrm{Im}}\,s_{2}=0$ . To analytically continue beyond $\alpha=1$ , we again move the contour around the pinching poles, picking up the residue at that pole. As described in the text, this happens again as $\alpha\to\frac{3}{2}$ [right] for two sets of poles in [green/purple].*

As illustrated in Figure 5, the “right” singularities generated by the factor $\Gamma\big[{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}s_{1}\,,\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}s_{2}\,,\,\alpha-\frac{1}{2}-s_{1}\,,\,\alpha-\frac{1}{2}-s_{2}\big]$ form horizontal and vertical lines in the ${\mathrm{Re}}\,s_{1}$ – ${\mathrm{Re}}\,s_{2}$ plane (in red and blue), while the “left” singularities generated by $\Gamma(2-2\alpha+s_{1}+s_{2})\Gamma(\tfrac{3}{2}-\alpha+s_{1}+s_{2})$ form diagonal lines (green and purple) that move towards the upper right as $\alpha\to\tfrac{3}{2}$ . As they move, they trap the integration contour (in yellow) between the lines of the right singularities and pinch it. As $\alpha\to\tfrac{3}{2}$ from $\alpha<1$ , the contour is first pinched by the $2-2\alpha+s_{1}+s_{2}=0$ singularity of $\Gamma(2-2\alpha+s_{1}+s_{2})$ . As before, we need to move the contour past this singularity. We do this first by replacing the $s_{1}$ integral with its residue and singularity-subtracted integral, and then similarly the $s_{2}$ integral. Fortunately, the residue at $s_{1}=-(2-2\alpha+s_{2})$ vanishes identically when we include the sum over shadows in (C.10), so we may write

$$ \begin{aligned} {}_{J}=\int\!\hskip 0.5pt\frac{\mathrm{d}s_{1}}{2\pi i}\frac{\mathrm{d}s_{2}}{2\pi i}&\,\,\Gamma^{/\{0\}}\big(2-2\alpha+s_{1}+s_{2}\big)\Gamma\big(\tfrac{3}{2}-\alpha+s_{1}+s_{2}\big)\\
\times&\,\,\Gamma\big[{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}s_{1}\,,\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}s_{2}\,,\,\alpha-\tfrac{1}{2}-s_{1}\,,\,\alpha-\tfrac{1}{2}-s_{2}\big]\mathcal{F}(s_{1},s_{2})\end{aligned}\,. $$

We can now continue this expression to $\alpha\to\tfrac{3}{2}$ . The integration contour is first pinched by the $\tfrac{3}{2}-\alpha+s_{1}+s_{2}=0$ singularity and then the $2-2\alpha+s_{1}+s_{2}=-1$ singularity. Treating each pinch in turn, we find that the residue again vanishes for the $2-2\alpha+s_{1}+s_{2}=-1$ singularity and so we are left with a single contribution from the $\tfrac{3}{2}-\alpha+s_{1}+s_{2}=0$ singularity,

$$ \displaystyle{}_{J} \displaystyle=-\pi\sec\pi\alpha\,\Gamma\big(\tfrac{1}{2}-\alpha\big)\mathcal{F}\big(0,\alpha-\tfrac{3}{2}\big)+\cdots \displaystyle=-\frac{\Gamma\big(\tfrac{1}{2}-\alpha\big)\csc\pi\Delta_{\sigma}\cos\pi(\alpha-\Delta_{\sigma})\csc\pi(2\alpha-\Delta_{\sigma})}{4(4\pi)^{\alpha-\frac{1}{2}}\Gamma(2-\Delta_{\sigma})\Gamma(2-2\alpha+\Delta_{\sigma})}+\cdots $$

where the $\cdots$ denote the remaining integral terms that are regular as $\alpha\to\frac{3}{2}$ . Taking $\alpha=(3-\epsilon)/2$ and $\epsilon\to 0$ , we are left with a suprisingly simple result for the full bubble diagram,

$$ [G^{\sigma}G^{\varphi}]_{J}\sim\frac{1}{8\pi^{2}\epsilon}\,, $$

which agrees with the divergence extracted from the exact result (A.15).

Before we move on, we should note that this derivation treated $\varphi$ as if it had mass $m_{\varphi}^{2}>\alpha^{2}-\frac{1}{4}$ so that we did not need to “straighten out” the $s_{1}$ contour to lie parallel to the imaginary axis. If the field is light enough, $m_{\varphi}^{2}<\alpha^{2}-\frac{1}{4}$ , we need to include a $1$ in our resolution (C.4) of the $\Delta_{\varphi}$ -dependent hypergeometric function that appears in $[\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}G^{\sigma}]_{J}$ . We are then left with an additional Mellin-type integral with an integrand that is proportional to $\Gamma\big(s_{2}+\tfrac{3}{2}-\alpha-\Delta_{\varphi}\big)\Gamma({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}s_{2})$ . As $\alpha\to\frac{3}{2}-\Delta_{\varphi}=\frac{3}{2}-\frac{1}{3}m_{\varphi}^{2}+\cdots$ , the left-most pole of $\Gamma\big(s_{1}+\tfrac{3}{2}-\alpha-\Delta_{\varphi}\big)$ and the right-most pole of $\Gamma({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}s_{1})$ pinch the integration contour. However, this does not contribute to the $\epsilon$ -divergence of $[G^{\sigma}G^{\varphi}]_{J}$ for two reasons. First, while the residue of $[\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\sigma}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}]_{J}$ at this pole is non-zero, it is exactly canceled by its shadow contribution from $[\mathcal{G}_{\smash{\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}]_{J}$ . Second, even if this residue did not vanish, this contribution is necessarily regular as $\epsilon\to 0$ , even if it diverges as $\epsilon\to 2m_{\varphi}^{2}/3$ . Thus, (C.13) applies when $\varphi$ is either light or heavy. Similar logic holds for other diagrams with light fields. Intuitively, this is obvious—straightening out this contour represents a modification in the deep IR, which should not affect the UV structure of these diagrams. We now turn our attention to the sunset diagram.

### C.3 The Sunset

Finally, let us turn our attention to the sunset diagram. It will be helpful to consider an arbitrary sunset diagram with fields $\varphi_{1}$ , $\varphi_{2}$ , and $\varphi_{3}$ with dimensions $\Delta_{1}$ , $\Delta_{2}$ , and $\Delta_{3}$ and propagators $G^{\varphi_{1}}$ , $G^{\varphi_{2}}$ and $G^{\varphi_{3}}$ , respectively,

$$ \displaystyle\big[G^{\varphi_{1}}G^{\varphi_{2}}G^{\varphi_{3}}\big]_{J} \displaystyle=\int\!\hskip 0.5pt\frac{\mathrm{d}s_{1}}{2\pi i}\,\frac{\mathrm{d}s_{2}}{2\pi i}\,\frac{\mathrm{d}s_{3}}{2\pi i}\,\,\Gamma\big(\tfrac{5}{2}-3\alpha+s_{1}+s_{2}+s_{3}\big)\Gamma\big(2-2\alpha+s_{1}+s_{2}+s_{3}\big) \displaystyle\times\,\,\Gamma\big[{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}s_{1}\,,\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}s_{2}\,,\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}s_{3}\,,\,\alpha-\tfrac{1}{2}-s_{1}\,,\,\alpha-\tfrac{1}{2}-s_{2}\,,\,\alpha-\tfrac{1}{2}-s_{3}\big]\mathcal{F}(s_{1},s_{2},s_{3})\,, $$

where we have defined the function

$$ \begin{aligned} \mathcal{F}(s_{1},s_{2},s_{3})&=\frac{(4\pi)^{-2\alpha-2}\sin\pi(\Delta_{1}+\Delta_{2}+\Delta_{3})}{2\sin\pi(\alpha-\Delta_{1})\sin\pi(\alpha-\Delta_{2})\sin\pi(\alpha-\Delta_{3})}\,\,\Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{0.0pt}{0}{J + 3 \alpha- \frac{3}{2} - s_1 - s_2 -s_3}{J - \alpha+ \frac{5}{2} + s_1 + s_2 + s_3}\right]\\
&\times\Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{0.0pt}{0}{\Delta_1 - \alpha+ \frac{1}{2} + s_1}{\Delta_1 - \alpha+ \frac{1}{2} - s_1}\right]\Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{0.0pt}{0}{\Delta_2 - \alpha+ \frac{1}{2} + s_2}{\Delta_2 - \alpha+ \frac{1}{2} - s_2}\right]\Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{0.0pt}{0}{\Delta_3 - \alpha+ \frac{1}{2} + s_3}{\Delta_3 - \alpha+ \frac{1}{2} - s_3}\right]+\cdots\end{aligned}\,, $$

where the $\cdots$ denote the other 7 permutations generated by taking $\Delta_{i}\to\bar{\Delta}_{i}=2\alpha-\Delta_{i}$ for $i=1,2,3$ . The integral over $\zeta$ from which this is derived naively converges only when $\alpha<\frac{5}{6}$ , and so we must analytically continue (C.14) from $\alpha<\frac{5}{6}$ towards $\alpha\to\frac{3}{2}$ in order to extract its singularity structure as $\epsilon\to 0$ .

The first singularity we encounter is at at $\frac{5}{2}-3\alpha+s_{1}+s_{2}+s_{3}=0$ as $\alpha\to\frac{5}{6}$ . Fortunately, like with the bubble diagram, the residue of the integrand at $s_{1}=3\alpha-\frac{5}{2}-s_{2}-s_{3}$ vanishes identically once we include the shadow contributions and so $[G^{\varphi_{1}}G^{\varphi_{2}}G^{\varphi_{3}}]_{J}$ is actually regular as $\alpha\to\frac{5}{6}$ . The first true singularity we encounter is from $2-2\alpha+s_{1}+s_{2}+s_{3}=0$ as $\alpha\to 1$ , in which we can write

$$ \begin{aligned} &\big[G^{\varphi_{1}}G^{\varphi_{2}}G^{\varphi_{3}}\big]_{J}=\Gamma(2-2\alpha)\Gamma\big(\alpha-\tfrac{1}{2}\big)^{2}\Gamma\big(\tfrac{3}{2}-\alpha\big)\Gamma\big(\tfrac{1}{2}-\alpha\big)\mathcal{F}(0,0,2\alpha-2)\\
&+\int\!\hskip 0.5pt\frac{\mathrm{d}s_{3}}{2\pi i}\,\,\Gamma^{/\{0\}}\big(2-2\alpha+s_{3}\big)\Gamma\big(\tfrac{3}{2}-\alpha+s_{3}\big)\\
&\qquad\quad\,\times\,\Gamma\big[{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}s_{3}\,,\,\alpha-\tfrac{1}{2}-s_{3}\,,\,\alpha-\tfrac{1}{2}\,,\,\tfrac{1}{2}-\alpha\big]\mathcal{F}(0,2\alpha-2-s_{3},s_{3})\\
&+\int\!\hskip 0.5pt\frac{\mathrm{d}s_{2}}{2\pi i}\,\frac{\mathrm{d}s_{3}}{2\pi i}\,\,\Gamma^{/\{0\}}(2-2\alpha+s_{2}+s_{3})\Gamma\big(\tfrac{3}{2}-\alpha+s_{2}+s_{3}\big)\\
&\qquad\qquad\,\,\,\,\,\times\,\Gamma\big[{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}s_{2}\,,\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}s_{3}\,,\,\alpha-\tfrac{1}{2}-s_{2}\,,\,\alpha-\tfrac{1}{2}\big]\mathcal{F}\big(2\alpha-2-s_{2}-s_{3},s_{2},s_{3}\big)\\
&+\int\!\hskip 0.5pt\frac{\mathrm{d}s_{1}}{2\pi i}\,\frac{\mathrm{d}s_{2}}{2\pi i}\,\frac{\mathrm{d}s_{3}}{2\pi i}\,\,\Gamma^{/\{0\}}\big(\tfrac{5}{2}-3\alpha+s_{1}+s_{2}+s_{3}\big)\Gamma^{/\{0\}}\big(2-2\alpha+s_{1}+s_{2}+s_{3}\big)\\
&\qquad\qquad\qquad\,\,\,\,\times\,\Gamma\big[{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}s_{1}\,,\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}s_{2}\,,\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}s_{3}\,,\,\alpha-\tfrac{1}{2}-s_{1}\,,\,\alpha-\tfrac{1}{2}-s_{2}\,,\,\alpha-\tfrac{1}{2}-s_{3}\big]\mathcal{F}(s_{1},s_{2},s_{3})\,\end{aligned}\,. $$

The next relevant singularity is from $\frac{5}{2}-3\alpha+s_{1}+s_{2}+s_{3}={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1$ as $\alpha\to\frac{7}{6}$ , but fortunately there the residue at $s_{1}=3\alpha-\frac{7}{2}-s_{2}-s_{3}$ also vanishes identically. Finally, there are four singularities that become relevant as $\alpha\to\frac{3}{2}$ , those from $\frac{5}{2}-3\alpha+s_{1}+s_{2}+s_{3}={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}2$ , $2-2\alpha+s_{1}+s_{2}+s_{3}={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1$ , and $\tfrac{3}{2}-\alpha+s_{2}+s_{3}=0$ or $\tfrac{3}{2}-\alpha+s_{3}=0$ . Luckily, the residue of the fourth line in (C.16) vanishes identically at $s_{1}=3\alpha-\frac{9}{2}-s_{2}-s_{3}$ after including the shadows, and so we end up with six terms

$$ \displaystyle[G^{\varphi_{1}} \displaystyle G^{\varphi_{2}}G^{\varphi_{3}}]_{J}=\Gamma\big(\tfrac{3}{2}-\alpha\big)\Gamma\big(\alpha-\tfrac{1}{2}\big)\Gamma\big(\tfrac{1}{2}-\alpha\big)^{2}\left[\mathcal{F}\big(0,\alpha-\tfrac{1}{2},\alpha-\tfrac{3}{2}\big)+\mathcal{F}\big(\alpha-\tfrac{1}{2},0,\alpha-\tfrac{3}{2}\big)\right] \displaystyle-\Gamma(3-2\alpha)\Gamma\big(\tfrac{5}{2}-\alpha\big)\Gamma\big(\alpha-\tfrac{3}{2}\big)\Gamma\big(\alpha-\tfrac{1}{2}\big)\Gamma\big(\tfrac{1}{2}-\alpha\big)\left[\mathcal{F}\big(0,1,2\alpha-3\big)+\mathcal{F}\big(1,0,2\alpha-3\big)\right] \displaystyle+\Gamma\big(\tfrac{3}{2}-\alpha\big)\Gamma\big(\alpha-\tfrac{1}{2}\big)^{2}\Gamma\big(2-2\alpha\big)\Gamma\big(\tfrac{1}{2}-\alpha\big)\mathcal{F}\big(0,0,2\alpha-2\big) \displaystyle-\Gamma(3-2\alpha)\Gamma\big({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\alpha-\tfrac{1}{2}\big)\Gamma\big(\tfrac{5}{2}-\alpha\big)\Gamma\big(\alpha-\tfrac{1}{2}\big)^{2}\mathcal{F}(0,0,2\alpha-3)+\cdots $$

where the $\cdots$ again denote the remainder integral terms that are regular as $\alpha\to\frac{3}{2}$ . In the limit $\epsilon\to 0$ , we have

$$ \displaystyle{}_{J}\sim-\frac{J(J+3)}{2(4\pi)^{4}\epsilon}-\frac{1}{64\pi^{4}\epsilon}+\mathcal{U}(\Delta_{1})+\mathcal{U}(\Delta_{2})+\mathcal{U}(\Delta_{3}) $$

where we have defined

$$ \mathcal{U}(\Delta)=-\frac{2(\Delta-1)(\bar{\Delta}-1)}{(4\pi)^{4}\epsilon}\left[\frac{1}{\epsilon}-\psi\big(\Delta-1\big)-\psi\big(\bar{\Delta}-1\big)+\log 4\pi{{\mathrm{e}}}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}\gamma_{\textsc{e}}}+\frac{3}{2}\right]\,. $$

Clearly the first divergence in (C.18) will be absorbed by the kinetic counterterm, while the remaining divergences will be absorbed by the mass counterterm.

