Throughout this paper, we have worked in Hubble units $H=1$ . We can restore dimensions by multiplying the sunset by a factor of $H^{2}$ . The flat space limit then corresponds to taking $H\to 0$ . All fields are “heavy” in this limit, and so $\Delta_{\varphi}=\alpha+i\sqrt{(m_{\varphi}/H)^{2}-\alpha^{2}}$ and $\Delta_{\varphi}\bar{\Delta}_{\varphi}=(m_{\varphi}/H)^{2}$ . With $H^{2}J(J+3)\to k^{2}$ , since both are eigenvalues of the Laplacian, we find that

$$ -\frac{J(J+3)}{2(4\pi)^{4}\epsilon}-\frac{1}{64\pi^{4}\epsilon}+3\,\mathcal{U}(\Delta_{\varphi})\to-\frac{k^{2}}{2(4\pi)^{4}\epsilon}-\frac{6m_{\varphi}^{2}}{(4\pi)^{2}\epsilon}\left[\frac{1}{\epsilon}+\log 4\pi{{\mathrm{e}}}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}\gamma_{\textsc{e}}}+\log H^{2}/m_{\varphi}^{2}+\frac{3}{2}\right] $$

and so (C.21) exactly reproduces the flat space divergence (C.20) when the renormalization scale is taken to be Hubble $\mu=H$ . This, however, is an automatic consequence of working in Hubble units, and so (C.21) exactly matches (C.20) in the flat-space limit $H\to 0$ .

## Appendix D UV Contributions in the Light Limit

In the main text, we argued that the UV contributions to the bubble and sunset diagrams were subleading as $m_{\varphi}$ for all $J$ , and we justify that statement in this appendix. As an example, we will study the UV contribution to the bubble diagram, given by

$$ [G^{\sigma}G^{\varphi}]_{J}^{\textsc{uv}}=[\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\sigma}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}]_{J}^{{\textsc{uv}}}+[\mathcal{G}_{\smash{\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}]_{J}^{{\textsc{uv}}}+[\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\sigma}}\mathcal{G}_{\smash{\bar{\Delta}_{\varphi}}}]_{J}^{{\textsc{uv}}}+[\mathcal{G}_{\smash{\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}}}\mathcal{G}_{\smash{\bar{\Delta}_{\varphi}}}]_{J}^{{\textsc{uv}}} $$

in the limit $\Delta_{\varphi}\to 0$ , where

$$ \displaystyle{}^{{\textsc{uv}}}_{J}=\mathcal{N}_{J,\Delta_{\sigma}\Delta_{\varphi}} \displaystyle\int_{1}^{\infty}\!\mathrm{d}\zeta\,\zeta^{J+\Delta_{\varphi}+\Delta_{\sigma}-1}\,{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$J+\alpha+ \frac{1}{2}, \,\, J+1$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 J + 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right] \displaystyle\times{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\Delta_{\sigma}, \,\, \Delta_\sigma- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 \Delta_\sigma- 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\Delta_{\varphi}, \,\, \Delta_\varphi- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 \Delta_\varphi- 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right] $$

and the other terms in (D.1) are just (D.2) with the dimensions appropriately replaced with their shadows. A similar analysis holds for the sunset diagrams, though since it is virtually identical to the bubble we will not repeat it.

The main trouble with naively expanding (D.2) in $\Delta_{\varphi}$ is that, as $\zeta\to\infty$ , all three hypergeometric functions conspire so that the integrand behaves as $\zeta^{2\alpha-3}$ as $\zeta\to\infty$ . Of course, this integral diverges as $\alpha\to\frac{3}{2}$ but, as discussed in the previous section, we will assume that we have already regularized these integrals using dimensional regularization. But, it is important to expand this integral in a way that does not disturb its structure as $\zeta\to\infty$ . This can be accomplished by writing the integral as

$$ \displaystyle\mathcal{N}_{J,\Delta_{\sigma}\Delta_{\varphi}}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}1}[\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\sigma}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}]^{{\textsc{uv}}}_{J}= \displaystyle\Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\frac{1}{2} - \alpha\,,\, 2 \Delta_\varphi- 2 \alpha+ 1$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$\Delta_\varphi- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}\,,\, \Delta_\varphi- 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\right]\int_{1}^{\infty}\!\mathrm{d}\zeta\,\zeta^{J+\Delta_{\sigma}-1}\,{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$J+\alpha+ \frac{1}{2}, \,\, J+1$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 J + 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right] \displaystyle\times{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\Delta_{\sigma}, \,\, \Delta_\sigma- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 \Delta_\sigma- 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\Delta_{\varphi}, \,\, 2\alpha- \Delta_\varphi$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$\alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\frac{1}{\zeta}\,\right]\,+ \displaystyle\Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\alpha- \frac{1}{2}\,,\, 2 \Delta_\varphi- 2 \alpha+ 1$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$\Delta_\varphi\,,\, \Delta_\varphi- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}\right]\int_{1}^{\infty}\!\mathrm{d}\zeta\,\zeta^{J+\Delta_{\sigma}+\alpha-\frac{3}{2}}\,{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$J+\alpha+ \frac{1}{2}, \,\, J+1$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 J + 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right] \displaystyle\times{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\Delta_{\sigma}, \,\, \Delta_\sigma- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 \Delta_\sigma- 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\frac{1}{2} - \Delta_\varphi+ \alpha, \,\, \frac{1}{2} + \Delta_\varphi- \alpha$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$\frac{3}{2} - \alpha$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\frac{1}{\zeta}\,\right]\,. $$

Both integrals are now well-behaved if we expand in $\Delta_{\varphi}$ and the second line is clearly subleading to the first as $\Delta_{\varphi}\to 0$ , since in this limit $1/\Gamma(\Delta_{\varphi})\sim\Delta_{\varphi}$ .

To leading order in $\Delta_{\varphi}$ , we can thus approximate this UV contribution as

$$ \displaystyle{}^{{\textsc{uv}}}_{J}\approx\mathcal{N}_{J,\Delta_{\sigma}\Delta_{\varphi}}\int_{1}^{\infty}\!\mathrm{d}\zeta \displaystyle\zeta^{J+\Delta_{\sigma}-1}\,{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$J+\alpha+ \frac{1}{2}, \,\, J+1$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 J + 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\Delta_{\varphi}, \,\, \Delta_\varphi- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 \Delta_\varphi- 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]\,, $$

which is indeed the original expression (D.2) with $\Delta_{\varphi}=0$ . The benefit of (D.3) is that it makes this approximation well-controlled, as the subleading terms are clearly of order $\mathcal{O}(1)$ and we can use it to construct a controlled series expansion in $\Delta_{\varphi}$ .

Combining this with the IR contribution, $[\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\sigma}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}]_{J}=[\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\sigma}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}]^{{\textsc{ir}}}_{J}+[\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\sigma}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}]^{{\textsc{uv}}}_{J}$ yields

$$ \displaystyle[\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\sigma}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}]_{J}\approx\frac{\Gamma(\alpha)\,\mathcal{N}_{J,\Delta_{\sigma}}}{4\pi^{\alpha+1}\Delta_{\varphi}}\int_{0}^{1}\!\mathrm{d}\zeta\,\zeta^{J+\Delta_{\sigma}-1}\,\!\big(\zeta^{\Delta_{\varphi}}-1\big)\,{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$J+\alpha+ \frac{1}{2}, \,\, J+1$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 J + 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right] \displaystyle\times{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\Delta_{\sigma}, \,\, \Delta_\sigma- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 \Delta_\sigma- 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right] \displaystyle+\frac{\Gamma(\alpha)\mathcal{N}_{J,\Delta_{\sigma}}}{4\pi^{\alpha+1}\Delta_{\varphi}}\int_{0}^{\infty}\!\mathrm{d}\zeta\,\zeta^{J+\Delta_{\sigma}-1}{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$J+\alpha+ \frac{1}{2}, \,\, J+1$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 J + 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\Delta_{\sigma}, \,\, \Delta_\sigma- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 \Delta_\sigma- 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]\,. $$

To this we add $[\mathcal{G}_{\smash{\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}}}\mathcal{G}_{\Delta_{\varphi}}]_{J}$ , which is of the same order as $\Delta_{\varphi}\to 0$ , and find with (C.13) that

$$ \displaystyle{}_{J} \displaystyle\approx\frac{\Gamma(\alpha)}{4\pi^{\alpha+1}}\frac{1}{\Delta_{\varphi}}\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\left[\frac{\mathcal{N}_{J,\Delta_{\sigma}}c_{k}(J,\Delta_{\sigma})}{J+\Delta_{\sigma}+\Delta_{\varphi}+k}+\frac{\mathcal{N}_{J,\smash{\bar{\Delta}}_{\sigma}}c_{k}(J,\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma})}{J+\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}+\Delta_{\varphi}+k}\right]+\frac{1}{8\pi^{2}\epsilon} \displaystyle+\frac{\Gamma(\alpha)}{4\pi^{\alpha+1}}\frac{1}{\Delta_{\varphi}}\left[\frac{1}{(J+\Delta_{\sigma})(J+\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma})}-\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\left[\frac{\mathcal{N}_{J,\Delta_{\sigma}}c_{k}(J,\Delta_{\sigma})}{J+\Delta_{\sigma}+k}+\frac{\mathcal{N}_{J,\smash{\bar{\Delta}}_{\sigma}}c_{k}(J,\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma})}{J+\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}+k}\right]\right] $$

where $c_{k}(J,\Delta_{\sigma})$ are the series coefficients (2.30). Since $c_{k}\big({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}[\Delta_{\sigma}+k],\Delta_{\sigma}\big)=\delta_{k,0}$ , the second term is purely analytic in $J$ , and thus subleading near $J=\Delta_{\sigma}$ . Furthermore, this also implies that $c_{k}\big({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}[\Delta_{\sigma}+\Delta_{\varphi}+k],\Delta_{\sigma}\big)=\delta_{k,0}+\mathcal{O}(\Delta_{\varphi})$ and so, to leading order in $\Delta_{\varphi}\propto m_{\varphi}^{2}$ , the bubble drastically simplifies to

$$ [G^{\sigma}G^{\varphi}]_{J}\approx\left[\frac{\Gamma(\alpha+1)}{2\pi^{\alpha+1}}\frac{1}{m_{\varphi}^{2}}\right]\frac{1}{(J+\Delta_{\sigma}+\Delta_{\varphi})(J+\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}+\Delta_{\varphi})}+\frac{1}{8\pi^{2}\epsilon}\,. $$

A similar story applies to the sunset diagram discussed in §3.2.

